ain.luau\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/state.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/mod.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/sync.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/lifecycle.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/context.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/benchmarks.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/host_api.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/template.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/helpers.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/runtime/vm.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/errors.rs\\ncrates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/proxy.rs\\nmodules/backend/hyprland-wm/module.json\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncodex-loop.log:698:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' .planning/STATUS.md && sed -n '1,220p' .planning/log/README.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Status\\n\\n**Updated:** 2026-08-21\\n\\nThis page describes the present. History lives in [`log/`](log/); open work\\nlives in [`docs/BACKLOG.md`](../docs/BACKLOG.md).\\n\\n## Now\\n\\nThe first four Section 2 items are complete. Package commits publish\\nvalidated module trees into a read-only SHA-256 object store and activate\\ngeneration-stamped snapshots. Installed-graph and shell discovery resolve the\\nactive object set, while editable module trees remain the authoring/update\\nsource. Shell runtime instances and catalogs now come only from the validated\\nresolved graph, and invalid startup/reload candidates fail closed. Candidate\\nupdates explain normalized graph changes before commit, while frontend primary\\nentries and extension-point contributions are indexed incrementally with\\nmodule-scoped source diagnostics and retryable watch paths. Focused package and\\ncatalog tests are added; Cargo\\nexecution is currently blocked\\nbecause the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\\n\\nTrust-tier plumbing is now complete for the current installer boundary: lock\\nentries carry local/community/core/verified classification and detached\\nsignature metadata, `module.sig` signs a canonical module identity/version/\\ndigest payload, and root-graph `trustPolicy.keys` verify Ed25519 signatures\\nbefore install, staged update review, lock verification, or graph activation.\\nInvalid signatures and unknown keys fail closed with scoped diagnostics;\\nregistry key distribution remains a future integration.\\n\\nService payloads now live in a generation-stamped Rust store owned by each\\nfrontend script context. Shared Lua VMs no longer receive `__mesh_svc_*`\\nglobals or marker tables; proxy reads are capability-filtered and copy values\\nout of the store, service state is read-only, and the shell skips payload\\ndelivery to contexts without the matching read grant. The focused scripting\\ntests are present, but Cargo execution remains blocked by the unavailable\\n`sha2` registry cache and network access.\\n\\nThe guarded `scripts/codex-backlog-runner.sh` can process one unchecked backlog\\nitem at a time directly on `main`, using Codex's explicit unrestricted mode for\\nunattended turns by default (`CODEX_ALLOW_ALL=0` opts back into workspace-write),\\nrequiring one feature commit and rolling to a fresh Codex session at the\\nruntime-reported context threshold. It is intentionally blocked by any\\npre-existing dirty working tree unless `--allow-dirty` is explicitly supplied\\nto recover an interrupted turn.\\n\\nOn NetworkManager hosts, live Codex network-wait errors trigger a throttled\\nattempt to restore the most recently used saved Wi-Fi profile; set\\n`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0` to disable it.\\n\\nThe 16 section improvement audits now have a feature-level checkbox checklist\\nin `docs/BACKLOG.md`; implementation detail, evidence, and regression matrices\\nremain in each section's `improvements.md`.\\n\\nPackage install, update, uninstall, and rollback now share the core\\n`PackageTransaction` engine: one OS lock, a durable journal, staged candidates,\\nfilesystem snapshots, and crash recovery. The completed backlog item and its\\nvalidation record are in the 2026-08 log.\\n\\nUpdate planning now materializes every candidate and unchanged graph member in\\nthe transaction workspace, resolves external interface contracts and profile\\nclosure from that staged graph, and records approval-filtered capability\\ndecisions before any live checkout or replacement.\\n\\nPer-widget promotion is complete: an embedded retained widget can move between\\nits parent surface and an independent `xdg_toplevel` while retaining the shared\\ncomponent VM, state, paint, and input path. The backlog item and design record\\nare closed; validation details are in the 2026-08 log.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 1 is complete: module capability declarations now resolve\\nagainst a closed catalog and persisted root-graph approvals before activation;\\nrequired grants fail without approval, optional grants default to denied, and\\nfrontend/backend runtimes receive only the resolved grant set.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 2 is complete: shell operations now pass through one\\ntyped registry that validates payloads, required capabilities, and caller\\nconstraints. Raw shell events, locale writes, and popover helpers share that\\nauthorization path; malformed, unauthorized, unknown, and dropped requests\\nproduce structured diagnostics.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 3 is complete: shared settings field declarations now\\nenforce numeric bounds and canonicalize enum values before deserialization.\\nRejected values fall back per field, preserving valid siblings; the runtime\\nand LSP settings schema consume the same declarations.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 4 is complete: diagnostics are keyed by module,\\ninstance, and stable issue code with severity, timestamps, counts, and active/\\nresolved state. Health aggregates active issues without severity downgrades;\\ncollector registration replaces identities, unregister removes stale instance\\nstate, snapshots are deterministic, and backend recovery resolves lifecycle\\nissues explicitly.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 5 is complete: the unused foundation `EventBus` and its\\nmisleading publish/subscribe contract were removed from the workspace. Live\\nextension events continue through the typed scripting queue and shell service\\nrouter, leaving one operational event path.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 6 is complete: public debug inspection and profiling DTOs\\nnow derive the versioned telemetry wire shape, including the invalidation fields\\nthat had drifted out of the shell payload. The shell adds the schema version and\\nkeeps only overlay/controller state plus the established module-graph compatibility\\nadapter outside the DTO boundary.\\n\\nThe settings store now recovers from a successfully parsed non-object document by\\nusing an empty default store and retaining an error diagnostic at the document\\nroot. I/O and irrecoverable JSON parse failures remain fatal.\\n\\nModule settings namespaces now receive complete owner-registered schema snapshots\\nfrom the installed graph. Registration and graph replacement stage validation\\nbefore commit; invalid values are omitted from the runtime projection while raw\\nunknown or uninstalled namespaces remain available for lifecycle repair.\\n\\nSettings persistence now writes owner-only settings files through unique\\nsame-directory temporary files, syncing file data before rename and the parent\\ndirectory after rename. Concurrent writers no longer share a fixed temporary\\npath, and newly created settings directories are owner-only.\\n\\nRoot-level settings diagnostics now render their key paths without a leading\\nseparator when the namespace is empty, so unknown root keys are reported as\\n`shel` rather than `.shel`.\\n\\nEmpty XDG config, data, data-search, and runtime directory variables now fall\\nback to their platform defaults instead of producing relative paths. This\\ncovers default configuration lookup, host icon discovery, and the shell IPC\\nsocket location.\\n\\nAllocation profiling now reports as available only after the runtime observes\\nthe counting allocator handling an allocation, so enabling its feature without\\ninstalling that global allocator no longer advertises live counters.\\n\\nModule package boundaries now validate typed `@scope/name` identities and\\ncontained relative paths before filesystem use. Installed trees, manifests,\\nlocks, profiles, graph entries, installers, updates, and removals reject\\nsymlinks; recursive discovery, source scanning, and digest traversal use\\nvisited canonical directories and never follow links outside their roots.\\n\\nThe Section 11 Luau runtime and sandbox audit is complete. Its logical process\\ntree, confirmed findings, broader feature direction, and regression matrix are\\nin [`sections/11-luau-runtime-and-sandbox/improvements.md`](log/sections/11-luau-runtime-and-sandbox/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 12 rendering and paint audit is complete. Its end-to-end process\\ntree, confirmed correctness and safety findings, unconstrained render-frame\\ndirection, and regression matrix are in\\n[`sections/12-rendering-and-paint/improvements.md`](log/sections/12-rendering-and-paint/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 13 surface policy and configuration audit is complete. Its process\\ntree, role/settings/manifest findings, policy-compiler direction, and\\nregression matrix are in\\n[`sections/13-surface-policy-and-configuration/improvements.md`](log/sections/13-surface-policy-and-configuration/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 14 Wayland platform and presentation audit is complete. Its\\nend-to-end lifecycle tree, confirmed protocol, popup, SHM, region, input, and\\ntest-fidelity findings, transactional presentation direction, and regression\\nmatrix are in\\n[`sections/14-wayland-platform-and-presentation/improvements.md`](log/sections/14-wayland-platform-and-presentation/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 16 developer and authoring tools audit is complete. Its end-to-end\\nCLI/LSP process tree, package-transaction and path-safety findings, canonical\\nauthoring-snapshot direction, LSP protocol findings, and regression matrix are\\nin [`sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md`](log/sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 15 shell core and orchestration audit is complete. Its end-to-end\\ninstruction tree, confirmed activation, backend lifecycle, generation,\\nscheduler, watcher, failure-isolation, and shutdown findings, revisioned shell\\nactivation direction, and regression matrix are in\\n[`sections/15-shell-core-and-orchestration/improvements.md`](log/sections/15-shell-core-and-orchestration/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Nix development shell now recommends the optimized release shell command,\\nkeeps the debug command explicit for debugging, and the shell logs its Cargo\\nbuild profile at startup. Production paints no longer build the focused proof\\nsnapshot; the proof remains available to the shell regression tests.\\n\\nAnimation-only frames now reuse the targeted restyle decision and skip an\\nunrelated full style walk when no interaction state changed. Slider `change`\\nand `release` handlers now use narrow script invalidation plus retained slider\\nrepainting instead of forcing a full tree rebuild.\\n\\nAnimation transition state and previous visual snapshots now key on stable\\n`NodeId`s. The transition pass no longer allocates a mesh-key string and then\\nclones it into a live set for every node; authored keyframe names remain\\nstring-based where they are part of the CSS-facing identity.\\n\\nShowing and hiding a surface is one clean map/unmap on the wire. A layer-surface\\nconfigure is never sent carrying a zero dimension no anchor backs — the\\nprotocol has to clamp those (1x1 at the anchor corner for a floating surface),\\nwhich is what made Settings collapse to a sliver and pop back on every show and\\nhide. Reopening also works at all again: hiding unmaps the surface and returns\\nit to the unconfigured state, and the render loop no longer waits for a\\nconfigure event before sending the configure that provokes it. Measured live\\nagainst Hyprland: five open/close cycles, one `openlayer`/`closelayer` pair and\\none 920x900 configure each, zero protocol clamps.\\n\\nSettings opening and closing has one idempotent visibility lifecycle. A\\nduplicate show cannot replay the entrance, a duplicate hide cannot shortcut an\\nactive exit, and the navigation launcher suppresses Quick Settings while the\\nfull Settings surface is being opened. Focused navigation and shell lifecycle\\nregressions pass.\\n\\nHyprland socket events now update provider state field-by-field instead of\\nrunning three `hyprctl` queries for every title change. Under a 10 Hz live\\nterminal-title workload with Settings scrolling, combined shell-plus-child CPU\\nfell from 66.8% to 15.9% of one core (4.20x); title events themselves now spawn\\nnothing, emit no redundant workspace event, and log below the default info\\nlevel. The 2-second full-state safety poll remains.\\n\\nGeometry-only retained updates now recompute only the layout fingerprint.\\nStyle and attribute hashes, child ids, and state are reused for clean nodes\\nthat moved through layout propagation, while authoritative dirty roots retain\\nthe full diff. The isolated 256-row release gate improves from\\n192.294–193.961ms to 62.866–62.967ms across three runs (3.06–3.08x).\\n\\nService-backed scroll and drag commands now use a 100ms backend-command budget\\nfor coalescable setters. Navigation brightness scroll emits the absolute\\ncoalescable setter; successful audio writes rely on live streams and optimistic\\n# Log\\n\\nDated history. **Append-only** — entries are never edited after the fact.\\n\\nAn entry that turns out to be wrong is corrected by a *new* entry that says so\\nand links back. Past entries hold the baselines that later work is measured\\nagainst; rewriting one destroys the comparison that made it worth keeping.\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n| File | Covers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `YYYY-MM.md` | The running work log. One file per month, newest entry at the top. |\\n| [`performance-log.md`](performance-log.md) | Performance history back to 2026-07-02, and the rejected-experiments table. |\\n| [`sections.md`](sections.md) | The current package-oriented concern map. Section letters (A–V) in older records are historical references. |\\n| [`backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md`](backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md) | Verbatim snapshot of `docs/BACKLOG.md` before the 2026-07-28 split. Holds the long progress narratives for items that are still open. |\\n| `<topic>-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | A single completed effort large enough to deserve its own file, e.g. `interaction-identity-2026-07-28.md`. |\\n\\nPerformance work goes in `performance-log.md`; everything else goes in the\\nmonthly file. When a monthly entry would run past roughly a screen, give it its\\nown topic file and leave a one-line pointer in the month.\\n\\n## Entry format\\n\\n```markdown\\n## 2026-07-28 — short title\\n\\n`commit-sha` · area: style resolution\\n\\nWhat changed, in a few sentences. What it means for someone reading this in six\\nmonths and deciding whether to touch the same code.\\n\\n**Measured.** Release, 456-node representative tree, three interleaved runs:\\n0.416–0.424ms before versus 0.390–0.397ms after (1.05–1.09x). Gated as\\n`shared_theme_defaults_speedup`.\\n\\n**Left open.** Typed property values and one-time token lowering.\\n```\\n\\nOnly the heading and the first line are required. Add the rest when there is\\nsomething real to put in them.\\n\\n## What earns an entry\\n\\n- Anything that closes a backlog item — the item leaves the backlog, its record\\n  lands here.\\n- Any performance change, with its measurement.\\n- Any approach that was tried, measured, and abandoned. These are the most\\n  valuable entries in the file: they stop the next attempt from re-running a\\n  known dead end. Add it to the rejected-experiments table in\\n  `performance-log.md` as well when it is performance work.\\n- Any decision that changes the shape of the system, with the reasoning.\\n\\nRoutine commits do not need an entry. `git log` already has them; the log is\\nfor the things a commit message is too small to hold.\\n\\n## Measurements\\n\\nA performance claim without its workload cannot be reproduced, and an\\nunreproducible number is worse than none — it gets trusted anyway. Record:\\n\\n- build profile (release, unless there is a reason it is not),\\n- workload shape and size (node counts, iteration counts, tree structure),\\n- before and after as ranges across repeated runs, not single numbers,\\n- the gate name, if the speedup is checked in CI.\\n\\nInterleave before/after runs when the effect is small enough that machine drift\\nbetween consecutive batches could account for it, and say that you did.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncodex-loop.log:700:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' '-- backlog headings --'; rg -n '\\\"'^##|''^###|''^- '\\\"\\\\\\\\[ \\\\\\\\]' docs/BACKLOG.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncodex-loop.log:701:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' '-- status --'; sed -n '1,260p' .planning/STATUS.md; printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' '-- current log tail --'; sed -n '1,260p' .planning/log/2026-08.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"-- status --\\n# Status\\n\\n**Updated:** 2026-08-21\\n\\nThis page describes the present. History lives in [`log/`](log/); open work\\nlives in [`docs/BACKLOG.md`](../docs/BACKLOG.md).\\n\\n## Now\\n\\nThe first four Section 2 items are complete. Package commits publish\\nvalidated module trees into a read-only SHA-256 object store and activate\\ngeneration-stamped snapshots. Installed-graph and shell discovery resolve the\\nactive object set, while editable module trees remain the authoring/update\\nsource. Shell runtime instances and catalogs now come only from the validated\\nresolved graph, and invalid startup/reload candidates fail closed. Candidate\\nupdates explain normalized graph changes before commit, while frontend primary\\nentries and extension-point contributions are indexed incrementally with\\nmodule-scoped source diagnostics and retryable watch paths. Focused package and\\ncatalog tests are added; Cargo\\nexecution is currently blocked\\nbecause the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\\n\\nTrust-tier plumbing is now complete for the current installer boundary: lock\\nentries carry local/community/core/verified classification and detached\\nsignature metadata, `module.sig` signs a canonical module identity/version/\\ndigest payload, and root-graph `trustPolicy.keys` verify Ed25519 signatures\\nbefore install, staged update review, lock verification, or graph activation.\\nInvalid signatures and unknown keys fail closed with scoped diagnostics;\\nregistry key distribution remains a future integration.\\n\\nService payloads now live in a generation-stamped Rust store owned by each\\nfrontend script context. Shared Lua VMs no longer receive `__mesh_svc_*`\\nglobals or marker tables; proxy reads are capability-filtered and copy values\\nout of the store, service state is read-only, and the shell skips payload\\ndelivery to contexts without the matching read grant. The focused scripting\\ntests are present, but Cargo execution remains blocked by the unavailable\\n`sha2` registry cache and network access.\\n\\nThe guarded `scripts/codex-backlog-runner.sh` can process one unchecked backlog\\nitem at a time directly on `main`, using Codex's explicit unrestricted mode for\\nunattended turns by default (`CODEX_ALLOW_ALL=0` opts back into workspace-write),\\nrequiring one feature commit and rolling to a fresh Codex session at the\\nruntime-reported context threshold. It is intentionally blocked by any\\npre-existing dirty working tree unless `--allow-dirty` is explicitly supplied\\nto recover an interrupted turn.\\n\\nOn NetworkManager hosts, live Codex network-wait errors trigger a throttled\\nattempt to restore the most recently used saved Wi-Fi profile; set\\n`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0` to disable it.\\n\\nThe 16 section improvement audits now have a feature-level checkbox checklist\\nin `docs/BACKLOG.md`; implementation detail, evidence, and regression matrices\\nremain in each section's `improvements.md`.\\n\\nPackage install, update, uninstall, and rollback now share the core\\n`PackageTransaction` engine: one OS lock, a durable journal, staged candidates,\\nfilesystem snapshots, and crash recovery. The completed backlog item and its\\nvalidation record are in the 2026-08 log.\\n\\nUpdate planning now materializes every candidate and unchanged graph member in\\nthe transaction workspace, resolves external interface contracts and profile\\nclosure from that staged graph, and records approval-filtered capability\\ndecisions before any live checkout or replacement.\\n\\nPer-widget promotion is complete: an embedded retained widget can move between\\nits parent surface and an independent `xdg_toplevel` while retaining the shared\\ncomponent VM, state, paint, and input path. The backlog item and design record\\nare closed; validation details are in the 2026-08 log.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 1 is complete: module capability declarations now resolve\\nagainst a closed catalog and persisted root-graph approvals before activation;\\nrequired grants fail without approval, optional grants default to denied, and\\nfrontend/backend runtimes receive only the resolved grant set.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 2 is complete: shell operations now pass through one\\ntyped registry that validates payloads, required capabilities, and caller\\nconstraints. Raw shell events, locale writes, and popover helpers share that\\nauthorization path; malformed, unauthorized, unknown, and dropped requests\\nproduce structured diagnostics.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 3 is complete: shared settings field declarations now\\nenforce numeric bounds and canonicalize enum values before deserialization.\\nRejected values fall back per field, preserving valid siblings; the runtime\\nand LSP settings schema consume the same declarations.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 4 is complete: diagnostics are keyed by module,\\ninstance, and stable issue code with severity, timestamps, counts, and active/\\nresolved state. Health aggregates active issues without severity downgrades;\\ncollector registration replaces identities, unregister removes stale instance\\nstate, snapshots are deterministic, and backend recovery resolves lifecycle\\nissues explicitly.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 5 is complete: the unused foundation `EventBus` and its\\nmisleading publish/subscribe contract were removed from the workspace. Live\\nextension events continue through the typed scripting queue and shell service\\nrouter, leaving one operational event path.\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 6 is complete: public debug inspection and profiling DTOs\\nnow derive the versioned telemetry wire shape, including the invalidation fields\\nthat had drifted out of the shell payload. The shell adds the schema version and\\nkeeps only overlay/controller state plus the established module-graph compatibility\\nadapter outside the DTO boundary.\\n\\nThe settings store now recovers from a successfully parsed non-object document by\\nusing an empty default store and retaining an error diagnostic at the document\\nroot. I/O and irrecoverable JSON parse failures remain fatal.\\n\\nModule settings namespaces now receive complete owner-registered schema snapshots\\nfrom the installed graph. Registration and graph replacement stage validation\\nbefore commit; invalid values are omitted from the runtime projection while raw\\nunknown or uninstalled namespaces remain available for lifecycle repair.\\n\\nSettings persistence now writes owner-only settings files through unique\\nsame-directory temporary files, syncing file data before rename and the parent\\ndirectory after rename. Concurrent writers no longer share a fixed temporary\\npath, and newly created settings directories are owner-only.\\n\\nRoot-level settings diagnostics now render their key paths without a leading\\nseparator when the namespace is empty, so unknown root keys are reported as\\n`shel` rather than `.shel`.\\n\\nEmpty XDG config, data, data-search, and runtime directory variables now fall\\nback to their platform defaults instead of producing relative paths. This\\ncovers default configuration lookup, host icon discovery, and the shell IPC\\nsocket location.\\n\\nAllocation profiling now reports as available only after the runtime observes\\nthe counting allocator handling an allocation, so enabling its feature without\\ninstalling that global allocator no longer advertises live counters.\\n\\nModule package boundaries now validate typed `@scope/name` identities and\\ncontained relative paths before filesystem use. Installed trees, manifests,\\nlocks, profiles, graph entries, installers, updates, and removals reject\\nsymlinks; recursive discovery, source scanning, and digest traversal use\\nvisited canonical directories and never follow links outside their roots.\\n\\nThe Section 11 Luau runtime and sandbox audit is complete. Its logical process\\ntree, confirmed findings, broader feature direction, and regression matrix are\\nin [`sections/11-luau-runtime-and-sandbox/improvements.md`](log/sections/11-luau-runtime-and-sandbox/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 12 rendering and paint audit is complete. Its end-to-end process\\ntree, confirmed correctness and safety findings, unconstrained render-frame\\ndirection, and regression matrix are in\\n[`sections/12-rendering-and-paint/improvements.md`](log/sections/12-rendering-and-paint/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 13 surface policy and configuration audit is complete. Its process\\ntree, role/settings/manifest findings, policy-compiler direction, and\\nregression matrix are in\\n[`sections/13-surface-policy-and-configuration/improvements.md`](log/sections/13-surface-policy-and-configuration/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 14 Wayland platform and presentation audit is complete. Its\\nend-to-end lifecycle tree, confirmed protocol, popup, SHM, region, input, and\\ntest-fidelity findings, transactional presentation direction, and regression\\nmatrix are in\\n[`sections/14-wayland-platform-and-presentation/improvements.md`](log/sections/14-wayland-platform-and-presentation/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 16 developer and authoring tools audit is complete. Its end-to-end\\nCLI/LSP process tree, package-transaction and path-safety findings, canonical\\nauthoring-snapshot direction, LSP protocol findings, and regression matrix are\\nin [`sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md`](log/sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Section 15 shell core and orchestration audit is complete. Its end-to-end\\ninstruction tree, confirmed activation, backend lifecycle, generation,\\nscheduler, watcher, failure-isolation, and shutdown findings, revisioned shell\\nactivation direction, and regression matrix are in\\n[`sections/15-shell-core-and-orchestration/improvements.md`](log/sections/15-shell-core-and-orchestration/improvements.md);\\nthe resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\\n\\nThe Nix development shell now recommends the optimized release shell command,\\nkeeps the debug command explicit for debugging, and the shell logs its Cargo\\nbuild profile at startup. Production paints no longer build the focused proof\\nsnapshot; the proof remains available to the shell regression tests.\\n\\nAnimation-only frames now reuse the targeted restyle decision and skip an\\nunrelated full style walk when no interaction state changed. Slider `change`\\nand `release` handlers now use narrow script invalidation plus retained slider\\nrepainting instead of forcing a full tree rebuild.\\n\\nAnimation transition state and previous visual snapshots now key on stable\\n`NodeId`s. The transition pass no longer allocates a mesh-key string and then\\nclones it into a live set for every node; authored keyframe names remain\\nstring-based where they are part of the CSS-facing identity.\\n\\nShowing and hiding a surface is one clean map/unmap on the wire. A layer-surface\\nconfigure is never sent carrying a zero dimension no anchor backs — the\\nprotocol has to clamp those (1x1 at the anchor corner for a floating surface),\\nwhich is what made Settings collapse to a sliver and pop back on every show and\\nhide. Reopening also works at all again: hiding unmaps the surface and returns\\nit to the unconfigured state, and the render loop no longer waits for a\\nconfigure event before sending the configure that provokes it. Measured live\\nagainst Hyprland: five open/close cycles, one `openlayer`/`closelayer` pair and\\none 920x900 configure each, zero protocol clamps.\\n\\nSettings opening and closing has one idempotent visibility lifecycle. A\\nduplicate show cannot replay the entrance, a duplicate hide cannot shortcut an\\nactive exit, and the navigation launcher suppresses Quick Settings while the\\nfull Settings surface is being opened. Focused navigation and shell lifecycle\\nregressions pass.\\n\\nHyprland socket events now update provider state field-by-field instead of\\nrunning three `hyprctl` queries for every title change. Under a 10 Hz live\\nterminal-title workload with Settings scrolling, combined shell-plus-child CPU\\nfell from 66.8% to 15.9% of one core (4.20x); title events themselves now spawn\\nnothing, emit no redundant workspace event, and log below the default info\\nlevel. The 2-second full-state safety poll remains.\\n\\nGeometry-only retained updates now recompute only the layout fingerprint.\\nStyle and attribute hashes, child ids, and state are reused for clean nodes\\nthat moved through layout propagation, while authoritative dirty roots retain\\nthe full diff. The isolated 256-row release gate improves from\\n192.294–193.961ms to 62.866–62.967ms across three runs (3.06–3.08x).\\n\\nService-backed scroll and drag commands now use a 100ms backend-command budget\\nfor coalescable setters. Navigation brightness scroll emits the absolute\\ncoalescable setter; successful audio writes rely on live streams and optimistic\\nstate instead of synchronous read-backs, while brightness publishes the\\nrequested level and lets its safety poll confirm rounding or clamping. The\\noriginal live capture remains the baseline; mocked command-path regressions\\ncover the pacing, stream read-back suppression, and optimistic state paths.\\n\\nA live capture against the running shell reframes the frame-cost work below:\\nthe render loop is not what costs. At rest the shell process uses 0.20% of one\\ncore, while its polling subprocesses (`hyprctl`, `wpctl`, `brightnessctl`, ~3\\n`fork`+`exec` per second) use 6.40% — 32x the rest of the shell, almost all of\\nit dynamic-linker process startup. Hyprland's own CPU is unchanged by the bar.\\nPer-frame render cost still matters under interaction, but it is not the idle\\ncost, and no idle frame-rate problem is reproducible.\\n\\nThe always-on navigation bar now has a frame-cost baseline of its own\\n(`navigation_frame_cost_profile`), alongside the existing Appearance profile.\\nSettled at 1920x56 with 107 nodes it costs 0.41ms for a plain repaint, 1.36ms\\nfor a pointer move, 1.60ms for a service poll nothing on the bar reads, and\\n2.58ms for one it does. Sampling puts ~27% of a steady-state frame in the\\nallocator and under 1% in Skia's pixel fill, so the software rasterizer is not\\nthe limit — per-frame `String` allocation and hashing in shell bookkeeping is.\\nTransition identity is now `NodeId`-keyed; remaining animation string work is\\nlimited to authored keyframe names. Measurements are in the performance log.\\n\\nBackground service polls no longer re-instantiate frontend components that\\ncannot observe them. Read capability is per module, so every component instance\\nof a module receives every payload the module may read; a runtime whose\\nrecorded template read sets show the update reaches it through neither the\\nservice proxy, the service state member, nor `last_service_update` now takes\\nthe value non-reactively. Declaring `render()` no longer disables the selective\\nservice build either — the hooks run before the frame snapshots its dirty\\nflags, and the gate is what they actually wrote. A Settings Appearance frame\\ndriven by an unrelated audio poll went from 12.2–12.4ms to 8.2–8.6ms.\\n\\nThe existing settings device page now has a typed `mesh.device` provider. The\\nnew `@mesh/device-info` Linux backend reports OS identity, kernel and\\narchitecture, desktop session, CPU, memory, graphics, hostname, and uptime\\nthrough the generic backend runtime; optional `lspci` support enriches graphics\\ndetails without making the provider unavailable.\\n\\nAuthor-declared visual customization is now live as a module-system feature.\\n-- current log tail --\\n# August 2026\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Isolate service snapshots from shared Luau globals\\n\\n`working tree` · area: service contracts and scripting isolation\\n\\nFrontend service payloads now use a per-context, Rust-owned store with\\ngeneration-stamped snapshots and capability filtering. Interface proxies read\\ncopied fields from that store, reject state writes, and no longer expose\\n`__mesh_svc_*` payload or fingerprint tables through shared Lua globals. Shell\\npayload delivery now skips contexts without the matching read capability.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\\nfocused scripting regression could not start because the local registry cache\\nlacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable; `cargo test --offline` reports\\nthe same missing dependency.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Verify signed module provenance before activation\\n\\n`working tree` · area: module provenance and candidate planning\\n\\nDetached `module.sig` records now sign one canonical module identity/version/\\ndigest payload. Root graphs may provide PEM Ed25519 trust anchors under\\n`trustPolicy.keys`; install, staged update planning, lock verification, and\\nresolved graph activation all use the same verifier. Signature files are\\nexcluded from module content digests, verified candidates receive the\\n`verified` tier, and unknown keys, malformed signatures, changed payloads, and\\nunsupported algorithms fail closed with `signature_invalid` diagnostics.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\\nfocused `cargo test -p mesh-core-module trust --locked` could not start because\\nthe local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Add trust-tier policy plumbing\\n\\n`working tree` · area: module provenance and activation policy\\n\\nLocks now retain a typed provenance tier and optional detached-signature\\nmetadata. Root graphs may set `trustPolicy.minimum`; graph construction blocks\\nlower-tier modules before dependency/provider/frontend activation, while CLI\\nand shell installs refuse candidates below that policy and staged update review\\nsurfaces trust-policy diagnostics even when revisions are unchanged. Existing\\nworkspaces remain permissive by default. Cryptographic signature verification\\nand registry key distribution remain open follow-up work.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\\nfocused trust-policy regression could not start because `sha2` is absent from\\nthe local registry cache; offline execution reports the same missing\\ndependency.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Isolate frontend source indexing failures\\n\\n`working tree` · area: frontend component catalog\\n\\nFrontend catalog construction now stays separate from the resolved module\\ngraph. Primary `.mesh` roots and extension-point contributions compile as\\nindependent incremental entries, unchanged entries are reused by manifest and\\nwatched-source fingerprints, and a failed module or contribution is retained\\nas a scoped catalog diagnostic instead of aborting unrelated entries. Each\\ndeclared contribution source remains in the host watch set so an edit can\\nretry a previously invalid source without changing graph activation state.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\\nfocused catalog regression could not start because `sha2` is absent from the\\nlocal registry cache; offline execution reports the same missing dependency.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Explain candidate graph effects during dry runs\\n\\n`working tree` · area: package planning and CLI review\\n\\nResolved module graphs now expose a deterministic diff for additions, removals,\\nupdates, enable/disable transitions, provider changes, and effective profile\\nlayout/slot changes. Update planning compares the installed graph with the\\nfully staged candidate graph, and the CLI prints that diff alongside existing\\ngrant, contract-break, and dependency diagnostics before the transaction can\\ncommit. `--dry-run` therefore reviews the same normalized graph the runtime will\\nactivate without changing live state.\\n\\nVerification: formatting and diff checks pass. The focused graph-diff test\\ncould not start because `sha2` is absent from the local registry cache and\\nnetwork access is unavailable.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Make the resolved graph the runtime inventory\\n\\n`working tree` · area: module graph and shell discovery\\n\\nShell discovery now loads one validated `InstalledModuleGraph` candidate and\\nconstructs runtime module instances, provider registrations, settings schemas,\\nand frontend catalog inputs from that graph. It no longer discovers arbitrary\\nmodule manifests and treats a missing graph as an allow-all filter. Graph load\\nfailure retains the last-known-good runtime; startup with no valid graph\\nactivates no module. Duplicate dependency validation in the shell was removed\\nso the package graph is the normalized compatibility authority.\\n\\nVerification: formatting and diff checks pass. Cargo tests remain unavailable\\nbecause the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is disabled.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Publish immutable module activation generations\\n\\n`working tree` · area: module store and graph activation\\n\\nPackage install, update, uninstall, and rollback now publish validated module\\ntrees into a shared SHA-256 content-addressed store. Objects and generation\\nsnapshots are read-only after atomic publication; the lock generation activates\\nonly after the matching snapshot exists. Installed-graph and shell discovery\\nconsume the active immutable object paths when available, while editable module\\ntrees remain the authoring and local-edit source. Package transaction recovery\\nprotects the active-generation pointer.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\\nCargo tests could not start because `sha2` is absent from the local registry\\ncache and network access to crates.io is unavailable.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Recover saved Wi-Fi on Codex network waits\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe backlog runner now reacts to live Codex `waiting for network` errors when\\nNetworkManager is available: it enables Wi-Fi and attempts the most recently\\nused saved wireless profile, throttled to one attempt per 30 seconds. Recovery\\nis best-effort, does not handle credentials, and can be disabled with\\n`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0`.\\n\\n## 2026-08-21 — Fall back from stale temporary-directory settings\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe backlog runner now skips a missing or unwritable `TMPDIR` and falls back to\\n`/tmp`, which keeps it usable after a Nix shell's temporary directory has been\\nremoved. It reports the fallback and fails with an explicit diagnostic only\\nwhen neither location can create the capture directory.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Fail closed graph candidates and retain live activation\\n\\n`working tree` · area: module graph activation and shell runtime reloads\\n\\nGraph-backed frontend catalogs now compile and mount only enabled modules from\\nthe validated installed graph. Discovery no longer registers module-owned\\ncontracts or providers before graph validation, invalid profile roots fail\\nclosed during startup, and graph replacement uses a prepare/commit boundary so\\nfailed reloads keep the last-known-good graph and active frontend/backend\\nruntime. Package removal also validates the replacement graph before removing\\nthe in-memory module or deactivating its live surface.\\n\\nVerification: the new last-known-good graph reload regression passed; focused\\ncatalog and frontend activation tests passed; `cargo check -p mesh-core-shell`\\nand formatting checks passed. The full shell library ran 674 passed, 6 failed,\\nand 130 ignored; the six failures are the known hover, raster, inspector,\\nbackend-manifest, debug-layout-label, and theme-contract baseline failures\\nrecorded by the Section 15 audit. No performance claim is made.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Recover from non-object settings documents\\n\\nSection 01 foundation hardening is implemented. `SettingsStore::load_from` and\\n`SettingsStore::from_value` now turn successfully parsed non-object documents\\ninto an empty usable store with declared shell defaults, while retaining one\\nerror diagnostic at the document root. I/O and irrecoverable JSON parse errors\\nremain fatal.\\n\\nVerification: `mesh-core-config --lib` passed 35 tests;\\n`mesh-core-surface-config --lib` passed 28 tests; `cargo check --workspace`\\npassed with existing warnings. No performance claim is made.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Use current context usage for runner rollover\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe Codex `--json` turn usage is cumulative session accounting, so treating it\\nas current context occupancy produced impossible negative remaining percentages\\nafter long turns. The runner now reads the latest non-ephemeral rollout's\\n`last_token_usage.total_tokens` and `model_context_window`, honors an explicit\\n`CODEX_CONTEXT_WINDOW_TOKENS` override, and clamps the displayed percentage.\\nWhen the rollout signal is unavailable, it reports the limitation and starts a\\nfresh session conservatively instead of presenting a false context estimate.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Add explicit interrupted-turn recovery\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe backlog runner keeps its clean-tree guard as the default but now accepts\\n`--allow-dirty` for an explicitly requested retry after a canceled turn left\\nthe feature edits in place. The option reports the existing status and allows\\nthe next bounded turn to finish and commit that work; normal invocations still\\nrefuse to risk incorporating unrelated changes.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Stream concise Codex runner progress\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe backlog runner now tees Codex JSONL into its capture file while filtering\\nlive output to concise session, turn, command, error, and completion updates.\\nLarge command `aggregated_output` and agent-message bodies are retained for\\npost-turn parsing but are no longer dumped to the terminal when a run is\\ncancelled or completes.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Fix unattended Codex permission mode\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nThe backlog runner now defaults to Codex's explicit unrestricted execution flag\\nfor unattended turns. The previous `--sandbox workspace-write` plus\\n`--approve-for-me` combination is rejected by the installed CLI; the runner now\\nuses `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`, with `CODEX_ALLOW_ALL=0` as a\\nworkspace-write fallback and no incompatible flag combination.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Add guarded Codex backlog runner\\n\\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\\n\\nAdded `scripts/codex-backlog-runner.sh` and its output schema. The runner\\nselects the next unchecked backlog item, gives Codex one bounded feature turn,\\nrequires the item’s removal and planning log record, and refuses to continue\\nunless exactly one non-merge commit was created directly on `main`. It resumes\\nthe same Codex session between features while its reported usage remains above\\nthe configured rollover threshold, then starts a clean session at approximately\\n30% context remaining. The context window defaults to 1,050,000 tokens for the\\nGPT-5.6 family and is configurable for other models. No production MESH code\\nchanged.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Add feature-level section audit checklist\\n\\nThe 16 section `improvements.md` audits are now represented in the canonical\\n[`docs/BACKLOG.md`](../../docs/BACKLOG.md) as feature-level unchecked items,\\ngrouped by section. The checklist includes each audit finding and explicit\\nfeature-direction item; shipped Section 01 findings and audit regression\\nmatrices remain recorded in their existing log/status documents. No production\\ncode changed and no performance claim is made.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Version debug telemetry DTOs and close parity gaps\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 6 is implemented. Public debug inspection and profiling\\nDTOs now derive serde serialization; profiling stage and repaint enum labels\\nretain their established wire names, and the `mesh.debug` payload carries\\n`DEBUG_TELEMETRY_SCHEMA_VERSION`. Narrow invalidation fields (`narrow_path`,\\n`affected_node_count`, and `component.script_narrow`) are serialized and covered\\nby a shell regression test. Overlay/controller fields remain explicitly\\nhost-owned, while the module graph keeps its established nested wire shape via\\na compatibility adapter.\\n\\nVerification: `mesh-core-debug --lib` passed 2 tests; shell benchmark filtered\\ntests passed 16 with 26 ignored; profiling filtered tests passed 26; and debug\\nfiltered tests passed 24 with 2 existing dirty-tree failures. No performance\\nclaim is made.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Remove the unused foundation EventBus\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 5 is implemented by removing the unused\\n`mesh-core-events` workspace crate, shell field, and orphaned dependencies.\\nThe live system already routes extension publications through the scripting\\nqueue and typed shell/service router; no production caller used the foundation\\nbus. This removes the contract whose unknown-channel and send-failure errors\\nwere unreachable and whose payloads were not actually typed.\\n\\nVerification: `cargo check -p mesh-core-shell -p mesh-core-scripting` passed\\nunder `nix develop`; the lockfile contains no `mesh-core-events` package. No\\nperformance claim is made.\\n\\n## 2026-08-20 — Key diagnostics by module instance and issue lifecycle\\n\\nSection 01 improvement 4 is implemented. Diagnostics now keep stable issue\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncodex-loop.log:702:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' '-- backlog headings --'; rg -n '\\\"'^##|''^###|''^- '\\\"\\\\\\\\[ \\\\\\\\]' docs/BACKLOG.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"-- backlog headings --\\n24:## Shell features\\n26:## Section audit feature checklist\\n32:### 1. Core foundation contracts\\n38:### 2. Module system and installation\\n43:### 3. Service contracts\\n47:- [ ] Make service methods correlated awaitable transactions with call IDs, deadlines, cancellation, and terminal outcomes.\\n48:- [ ] Make optimistic state writes settle or roll back transactionally without overriding newer writes.\\n49:- [ ] Compile and enforce explicit read, event-subscription, and per-method operation policy at every service boundary.\\n50:- [ ] Replace additive registry mutation with one immutable generation-stamped resolved service catalog.\\n51:- [ ] Compile recursive named/array/optional/result schemas and enforce them for state, events, inputs, and outputs.\\n52:- [ ] Prepare provider output and commit provider selection only after validated readiness and initial state.\\n53:- [ ] Deliver generation-stamped availability transitions and reject stale state, event, and result messages.\\n54:- [ ] Diff executable consumer/provider contracts in both directions, including external candidate contracts and behavioral annotations.\\n55:- [ ] Canonicalize contract identities and make graph, runtime, LSP, codegen, docs, and diagnostics consume one compiled artifact.\\n56:- [ ] Remove the transitional service registry after remaining callers migrate to the generation-stamped catalog.\\n57:- [ ] Introduce an immutable compiled contract containing schemas, operation policy, fingerprints, and declaration provenance.\\n58:- [ ] Generate Luau consumer types, provider stubs, mocks, and contract documentation from compiled contracts.\\n59:- [ ] Model optional service feature groups explicitly and negotiate them per provider, or remove the unsupported claim.\\n60:- [ ] Make consumer/provider compatibility classifications independently expressible rather than using one undirected label.\\n61:- [ ] Use one call transaction model for awaitable results, cancellation, deadlines, coalescing, tracing, and optimistic rollback.\\n63:### 4. Themes\\n67:- [ ] Build the theme catalog only from graph-authorized descriptors with scoped ownership, modes, and contained source handles.\\n68:- [ ] Resolve theme selections through catalog identities with symlink-safe, bounded, UTF-8-checked source opening.\\n69:- [ ] Prepare and atomically commit theme/profile/reload candidates while retaining the last-known-good snapshot on failure.\\n70:- [ ] Implement the base, selected pack/mode, owned module contribution, and sparse user override cascade with provenance.\\n71:- [ ] Make theme selection durable, fingerprinted, watchable, and revision-consistent across settings/profile changes.\\n72:- [ ] Share a restricted CSS parser/lowering and selector matcher between component and theme styles.\\n73:- [ ] Share keyframes across theme and component styles and resolve token dependencies, cycles, and inherited custom properties.\\n74:- [ ] Make the rendered theme snapshot authoritative for mode, color scheme, contrast, tokens, provenance, and revisioned events.\\n75:- [ ] Make theme snapshots immutable and reject duplicate or ambiguous identities for deterministic caching.\\n76:- [ ] Add explicit manual, follow-system, and scheduled theme mode policies through the same transaction coordinator.\\n78:### 5. Localization and i18n\\n82:- [ ] Replace the global translation pool with owner-scoped translators for templates, scripts, manifests, props, debug data, and settings UI.\\n83:- [ ] Implement an immutable normalized locale selection with BCP 47 parents, direction, complete fallback chain, and revision.\\n84:- [ ] Compile bounded typed message/plural/select entries independently with checked placeholders and shared formatting.\\n85:- [ ] Build one immutable catalog snapshot with atomic graph/profile/selection/reload commits and last-known-good recovery.\\n86:- [ ] Add targeted ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance, and duplicate precedence checks.\\n87:- [ ] Route locale changes through one durable revision-checked settings/profile transaction.\\n88:- [ ] Align `mesh.i18n`, `mesh.locale`, runtime/LSP ABI, and read/write capability enforcement.\\n89:- [ ] Centralize localized metadata and visible miss handling with owner, fallback, source, and snapshot revision.\\n90:- [ ] Open bounded catalogs through contained no-follow source handles and compile them off the shell thread.\\n91:- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/extraction/doctor tooling from the canonical graph, and implement or explicitly reject `<i18n>` blocks.\\n92:- [ ] Add manual, follow-system, and per-profile locale policies through the same prepare/persist/commit path.\\n94:### 6. Host resources and icon packs\\n98:- [ ] Build icon and font resources only from the prepared graph/profile and atomically retain the last-known-good resource snapshot.\\n99:- [ ] Preserve declared pack order and reject duplicate canonical IDs, aliases, and ambiguous ownership deterministically.\\n100:- [ ] Open module resource assets through contained, no-follow, bounded handles.\\n101:- [ ] Implement canonical semantic and dash-generalized icon fallback resolution with required/optional diagnostics.\\n102:- [ ] Preserve typed multicolor mappings, source color policy, and mapping provenance.\\n103:- [ ] Validate complete packs off the render thread and publish them atomically without partial registry state.\\n104:- [ ] Propagate resource revisions/fingerprints through negative lookup, glyph, font, text-layout, and renderer caches.\\n105:- [ ] Implement font-pack runtime resolution with logical roles, pack-qualified roles, faces, coverage, and typography tokens.\\n106:- [ ] Unify XDG icon/font discovery and lookup into one ordered, refreshable host catalog.\\n107:- [ ] Prepare resource parsing and asset handles away from shell/render threads with bounded cancellation.\\n108:- [ ] Generate runtime, CLI, LSP, doctor, and debug resource explanations from one effective snapshot.\\n109:- [ ] Add a resource coverage advisor for semantic vocabulary and font-script gaps without silently reordering user chains.\\n111:### 7. Component language\\n115:- [ ] Make local component imports module-relative, canonicalized, contained, and symlink-safe across compiler, watcher, and LSP paths.\\n116:- [ ] Resolve recursive imports by owner scope and canonical target, with collision and cycle diagnostics.\\n117:- [ ] Replace lossy block extraction with a span-preserving top-level parser that validates required, unique, attributed blocks and `<i18n>` policy.\\n118:- [ ] Parse interpolations and control-flow braces with a real lexer/parser that rejects malformed expressions and preserves spans.\\n119:- [ ] Add semantic validation linking `<props>`, `prop()` references, child props, visibility, types, and CSS domains.\\n120:- [ ] Select the highest valid value across prop/configuration layers while retaining invalid overrides only in diagnostics.\\n121:- [ ] Make JSON-to-prop conversion scalar-only until an explicit structured prop type exists.\\n122:- [ ] Normalize and validate `PropDef` constraints, units, options, tokens, and CSS-domain values once.\\n123:- [ ] Replace line-oriented script/import/symbol scans with one Luau lexer/parser and source metadata.\\n124:- [ ] Carry reliable source spans through component AST nodes, compiler errors, CLI diagnostics, and LSP output.\\n126:### 8. UI element core\\n130:- [ ] Normalize roles, names, descriptions, hidden state, ARIA aliases, focus, relationships, and visibility after child construction and publish the semantic snapshot.\\n131:- [ ] Use visible descendant text in accessible-name precedence and preserve hidden-child and locale behavior.\\n132:- [ ] Generate one typed pseudo-state table for indexing, mutation, matching, invalidation, diagnostics, and tests.\\n133:- [ ] Make compiler inheritance matching consume the actual element state.\\n134:- [ ] Make retained layout transactional, preserve last-known-good geometry, and retry after failure.\\n135:- [ ] Use one stateful input dispatcher with pointer capture, press-origin identity, activation semantics, focus eligibility, and invalidation output.\\n136:- [ ] Generate element types, source/runtime tags, contracts, events, attributes, style hooks, and accessibility defaults from one schema.\\n137:- [ ] Detect CSS custom-property cycles with structured diagnostics and specified invalid-value fallback.\\n138:- [ ] Preserve explicit/inherited property masks through retained style resolution and targeted restyle.\\n139:- [ ] Derive accessibility focus from canonical live focus state during snapshot generation.\\n140:- [ ] Include all shaping inputs and resource/measurer revisions in text measurement contexts and cache keys.\\n141:- [ ] Make popover placement tokens and trigger/surface relationships typed, validated, and observable across promotion.\\n142:- [ ] Introduce an immutable frame snapshot with phase stamps, semantic diffs, stable identities, and property-based invariant tests.\\n144:### 9. Interaction and motion\\n148:- [ ] Share visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and target filtering across interaction, rendering, focus, scrolling, tooltips, and accessibility.\\n149:- [ ] Use one affine transform/clip contract for hit testing, paint bounds, scrolling, and focus geometry.\\n150:- [ ] Prevent disabled and inert nodes, including descendants and captured targets, from receiving activation.\\n151:- [ ] Preserve keyframe progress across pause/resume and iteration-boundary changes.\\n152:- [ ] Add a `MotionPolicy` snapshot for reduced motion across transitions, keyframes, scrolling, inertia, tooltips, and surfaces.\\n153:- [ ] Consolidate focus, pointer capture, press origin, gesture ownership, and scroll ownership into one transaction.\\n154:- [ ] Preserve the previous discrete value during visibility transitions until the transition completes.\\n155:- [ ] Propagate validated per-keyframe easing through component parsing, shell state, and animation sampling.\\n156:- [ ] Keep interaction policy in a renderer-neutral frame/state-machine contract with typed decisions and dirty outputs.\\n157:- [ ] Implement `box-shadow` parsing with structured errors or reject it before the public animation API.\\n158:- [ ] Give animation instances stable identity and explicit replacement, cancellation, and reversal semantics.\\n159:- [ ] Introduce an `InteractionFrame` shared by input, state, style invalidation, layout, animation, paint, and semantics.\\n161:### 10. Frontend compiler and host\\n165:- [ ] Reject absolute, traversal, and symlinked frontend entrypoint/import paths outside the module root.\\n166:- [ ] Keep service payloads Rust-owned unless the consumer has the resolved read capability; event subscriptions alone must not expose state.\\n167:- [ ] Reload primary and contribution roots together as one atomic catalog generation.\\n168:- [ ] Validate contribution roots against interface requirements, availability, and version ranges.\\n169:- [ ] Validate root and nested expression scopes uniformly using parser/runtime symbol tables and source spans.\\n170:- [ ] Compile expressions once into shared semantics so preview, live, translation, and composition paths behave identically.\\n171:- [ ] Split the frontend host into a renderer-neutral ABI and shell adapters with typed capability-scoped effects.\\n172:- [ ] Build one reverse dependency graph over primary and contribution roots for invalidation.\\n173:- [ ] Scope local component aliases by owner and canonical source, rejecting collisions.\\n174:- [ ] Replace line-oriented Luau symbol discovery with parser/compiler metadata or remove it as a source of truth.\\n175:- [ ] Make expression scanning UTF-8 safe and diagnose non-ASCII input instead of panicking.\\n176:- [ ] Publish runtime props transactionally so Rust and Luau cannot diverge after a failed update.\\n177:- [ ] Preserve typed diagnostic categories and source spans through AST, compiler, shell, LSP, and debug paths.\\n178:- [ ] Prevent stale catalog rollback from overwriting a newer generation using a coordinator or compare-and-swap.\\n179:- [ ] Enforce distinct popover and overflow child-surface lifecycle, focus, dismissal, placement, and ownership semantics.\\n180:- [ ] Dispatch frontend `mount` and `unmount` hooks in every initialization, reload, deactivation, and replacement path.\\n181:- [ ] Contain expression/runtime failures with last-known-good trees or bounded error placeholders and actionable diagnostics.\\n182:- [ ] Publish normalized public-prop schemas and validate imported/contribution props at the import boundary.\\n183:- [ ] Reject local/module import alias collisions or resolve them through one typed import namespace.\\n184:- [ ] Introduce a coherent revisioned `FrontendFrame` boundary for tree, catalog, runtime, services, invalidation, diagnostics, and effects.\\n185:- [ ] Emit immutable compiled frontend revisions and authorize typed effects only when catalog/runtime revisions still match.\\n187:### 11. Luau runtime and sandbox\\n191:- [ ] Enforce one sandbox/resource policy for every Luau realm, including instruction, memory, output, queue, storage, and child-process budgets.\\n192:- [ ] Keep service state Rust-owned, generation-aware, and capability-filtered instead of exposing shared globals.\\n193:- [ ] Route every backend early return, callback failure, stop, and crash through idempotent cleanup and one terminal lifecycle record.\\n194:- [ ] Make unsubscribe-safe event iteration and independent subscriber failure reporting explicit.\\n195:- [ ] Give stream subprocesses stable identities, bounded queues, exit events, awaited reaping, and shutdown semantics.\\n196:- [ ] Move `mesh.exec` off the async backend loop and enforce cancellation, deadlines, executable policy, and output limits.\\n197:- [ ] Replace basename executable grants with canonical path/argument capability policy.\\n198:- [ ] Move default runtime storage to secure durable XDG state with permissions, quotas, atomic recovery, and revisioned writers.\\n199:- [ ] Make backend command dispatch typed, correlated, transactional, coalescable, generation-aware, and bounded.\\n200:- [ ] Gate event and side-effect queues through one typed authorization/resource boundary before acceptance.\\n201:- [ ] Represent locale as a host-owned per-context cell updated with the translation snapshot.\\n202:- [ ] Treat Luau `nil` writes as explicit deletions in Rust state and dependency invalidation.\\n203:- [ ] Restrict provider event publication to provider-owned handles and isolate subscriber callback failures.\\n204:- [ ] Preserve one stable backend `self` and event/storage handles for each runtime generation.\\n205:- [ ] Publish validated initial provider state before atomically marking the provider ready.\\n206:- [ ] Compile and swap a fresh Luau environment on reload, preserving only explicit durable storage.\\n207:- [ ] Stage backend top-level execution so host side effects cannot escape a failed `start(self)`.\\n208:- [ ] Bound command/event ingress, JSON depth/bytes, event counts, and aggregate runtime resource budgets.\\n209:- [ ] Separate effective read and control grants and enforce them through both proxy and shell paths.\\n210:- [ ] Reconcile changed poll intervals after every stream callback.\\n211:- [ ] Return recoverable host-installation errors instead of panicking during backend setup.\\n212:- [ ] Introduce a per-module `RuntimeSession` combining realm, host, resource broker, lifecycle, state, supervisor, health, backoff, and quarantine.\\n214:### 12. Rendering and paint\\n218:- [ ] Make `PixelCanvasSession` storage-safe so mapped drawing surfaces cannot outlive backing allocations.\\n219:- [ ] Lower asymmetric four-edge borders and four-corner radii correctly.\\n220:- [ ] Use one cumulative affine transform/clip model across paint, damage, blur, descendants, and interaction.\\n221:- [ ] Lower node opacity and blend mode as isolated compositing groups instead of per primitive.\\n222:- [ ] Include paint-order/topology changes in display-list generations and stable equal-z ordering.\\n223:- [ ] Unify dirty contracts and paint signatures for all content/style fields, including controls, text, icons, and variables.\\n224:- [ ] Include font/resource revisions in text style painting and all glyph/font/text cache keys.\\n225:- [ ] Make backdrop blur an explicit renderer/backend capability with validated fallback behavior.\\n226:- [ ] Use one fractional-scale rounding policy across layout, buffer, paint, copy, and protocol damage.\\n227:- [ ] Preserve release batch/barrier metrics in diagnostics and debug telemetry.\\n228:- [ ] Validate caller lineage before trusting generation shortcuts.\\n229:- [ ] Include resource revisions in retained-present decisions and cache invalidation.\\n230:- [ ] Preserve pending damage and avoid acknowledging unshown frames after presentation errors.\\n231:- [ ] Move resource decode and rasterization off the frame thread through a bounded resource broker.\\n232:- [ ] Enforce byte- and dimension-based cache budgets for decoded assets, fonts, glyphs, text, Skia, PixelBuffer, and SHM allocations.\\n233:- [ ] Introduce a typed frame paint plan with immutable inputs, topology, transforms, effect regions, replay spans, and exact logical/device damage.\\n235:### 13. Surface policy and configuration\\n239:- [ ] Enforce the author-only `promotable` guard for settings, IPC, and live role changes.\\n240:- [ ] Remove or formally protect `promotable` from user-overridable/ejected settings.\\n241:- [ ] Validate manifest surface enums and role constraints with canonical parsers before graph resolution.\\n242:- [ ] Include blur and window decorations in semantic presentation change detection.\\n243:- [ ] Route settings-driven role reloads through the same transactional transition supervisor as explicit promotion.\\n244:- [ ] Share role-field metadata across manifest diagnostics, settings validation, ejection, and protocol lowering.\\n245:- [ ] Preserve localization identity and distinguish effective values from overrides during configuration ejection.\\n246:- [ ] Use typed unmeasured/content/surface/wire extents at the shell/presentation boundary.\\n247:- [ ] Replace split surface field lists with a revisioned semantic policy diff and generation.\\n248:- [ ] Build the policy compiler producing declared contracts, effective snapshots, and typed transition plans.\\n250:### 14. Wayland platform and presentation\\n254:- [ ] Return typed create/configure/present/lost outcomes, cache only accepted generations, and retain damage for missing surfaces.\\n255:- [ ] Route close, dismiss, parent destruction, role replacement, and connection loss through one idempotent teardown supervisor.\\n256:- [ ] Replace incomplete presentation hashes and warm region caches with typed semantic diffs and object/configure/frame generations.\\n257:- [ ] Preserve click-grab seat and serial identity through input normalization, popup requests, and one-shot configuration.\\n258:- [ ] Gate popup reposition by negotiated protocol version and validate popup role, parent, identity, and reparenting.\\n259:- [ ] Use one authoritative logical extent for paint, buffer validation, viewport, regions, attach, and queries.\\n260:- [ ] Commit opaque, blur, and other surface-state changes even when pixel damage is empty.\\n261:- [ ] Separate frame-callback waiting from buffer-release backpressure and prevent hot retry loops.\\n262:- [ ] Make input ownership per seat and cancel pointer, touch, gesture, focus, and repeat transactions during teardown.\\n263:- [ ] Validate buffer length/stride/scale, propagate attach/region errors, expose connection loss, and reap clipboard children.\\n264:- [ ] Replace the recorder backend with a deterministic protocol-state lifecycle simulator plus a focused live compositor matrix.\\n265:- [ ] Add full pointer-button identity and IME/text-input-v3 support while preserving normalized Escape behavior.\\n266:- [ ] Build the transactional, capability-aware presentation engine with version reporting, reactive popups, multi-output membership, per-seat input, and shared SHM/dmabuf/GPU frame contracts.\\n268:### 15. Shell core and orchestration\\n272:- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one immutable activation plan and atomic runtime-generation commit.\\n273:- [ ] Reconcile backend enable/disable and every graph delta through the same activation coordinator.\\n274:- [ ] Tag backend tasks, bridges, messages, events, results, and restart deadlines with activation generations and provider epochs.\\n275:- [ ] Make frontend/backend stop, unmount, storage flush, worker join, and shutdown cleanup explicit and idempotent.\\n276:- [ ] Give detached workers owned wake handles and lifecycle guards that outlive the eventfd until join.\\n277:- [ ] Isolate component/runtime callback, tick, build, render, and reload failures with placeholders, diagnostics, and quarantine.\\n278:- [ ] Replace static startup watching with a managed generation-aware watch set and bounded polling fallback.\\n279:- [ ] Process CoreRequest effects through one fair bounded scheduler with causal budgets and cycle detection.\\n280:- [ ] Publish provider unavailable/recovery transitions from committed provider generations.\\n281:- [ ] Route control-plane writes through declared durable revisions and ordered settings/theme/locale effect batches.\\n282:- [