{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- latest log ---' && sed -n '1,260p' .planning/log/2026-08.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- locale mentions in status ---' && rg -n -C 2 'catalog|locale|snapshot|atomic|graph|profile' .planning/STATUS.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"--- latest log ---\n# August 2026\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Prepare graph locale catalogs before surface commit\n\n`working tree` · area: surface catalog lifecycle\n\nFrontend surfaces now compile graph catalogs into a fresh locale candidate and\nreplace their catalog Arc only after all catalog assets are readable. An asset\nread/parse failure retains the prior catalog snapshot while valid structured\nentries continue to produce per-entry diagnostics.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and\n`git diff --check` pass. The focused locale suite passed 9 tests before this\nshell-only wiring; workspace compilation remains blocked by pre-existing\nservice/module/scripting errors before the shell crate.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Establish immutable locale catalog snapshots\n\n`working tree` · area: catalog snapshot ownership\n\n`LocaleEngine` now stores catalogs behind a revisioned immutable\n`LocaleCatalogSnapshot` Arc. Catalog loads use copy-on-write preparation, and\ncallers can atomically replace the catalog pointer while readers retain the\nprevious last-known-good snapshot. The shell/graph/profile coordinator still\nneeds to adopt this seam; that remaining work stays open in the backlog.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n(9 tests), `git diff --check` passed. The final formatting check was retried\nafter the Nix daemon socket was temporarily unavailable; earlier focused\nformat checks pass for the same working tree.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Compile typed locale entries without sibling loss\n\n`working tree` · area: typed catalogs and shared message rendering\n\nLocale catalog loading now compiles bounded string, plural, and select entries\nindependently. Invalid keys, malformed placeholders, inconsistent variant\nplaceholders, unknown plural categories, and oversized entries produce\nper-entry diagnostics while valid siblings remain available. Rust translators\nshare interpolation and plural/select resolution, and Luau `t(key, values)`\nuses the same compiled entries. Graph catalog loading now accepts structured\nJSON instead of rejecting an entire file at the first plural object.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n(8 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n--check` pass. The scripting/shell workspace remains blocked by pre-existing\nclosure ownership and module graph compilation errors before shell compilation.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Normalize locale selection into revisioned snapshots\n\n`working tree` · area: locale selection and service state\n\nAdded immutable `LocaleSelection` snapshots with canonical BCP 47 casing,\nderived script/region parents, explicit terminal fallback, RTL/LTR direction,\nand monotonic revisions. Locale changes now replace the prior chain instead of\naccumulating stale locales; invalid runtime tags are rejected. The shell\npublishes the normalized chain, direction, and revision, and retained\ncomponents adopt the same selection snapshot.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n(7 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n--check` pass. Workspace compilation remains blocked by pre-existing errors in\nthe service, module, and scripting crates before shell compilation.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Replace global locale lookup with owner-scoped translators\n\n`working tree` · area: localization ownership and consumption\n\n`LocaleEngine` no longer merges module catalogs into a shared translation\npool. It now exposes explicit module and core translator handles; module\ntranslators resolve only their owner’s catalogs. Template variable stores,\nLuau i18n maps, manifest text, debug metadata, and generated composition prop\nsettings all use the corresponding owner-scoped handle. Existing keybind/debug\nfixtures were migrated to module-owned catalogs, and cross-module/core leakage\nis covered by locale tests.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n(4 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n--check` pass. The scripting/shell compile remains blocked by the pre-existing\n`InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Route theme mode policies through candidate preparation\n\n`working tree` · area: theme mode selection and settings\n\nTheme settings now support explicit `manual`, `follow_system`, and\n`scheduled` policies. A pure selector validates declared modes, matches system\ncolor-scheme metadata, resolves local clock schedules, and rejects malformed,\nunknown, or duplicate entries. Graph theme preparation and reload fingerprint\nchecks use that selector, so policy transitions enter the same candidate\ncomposition, invalidation, and revisioned event path as manual changes.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-theme` passed\n(23 tests), `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-config --lib` passed\n(41 tests), and formatting/diff checks pass. The focused shell suite remains\nblocked by the pre-existing `InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Make active theme snapshots immutable and identities deterministic\n\n`working tree` · area: theme snapshot identity and catalog registration\n\nThemeEngine now keeps an `Arc` of the active immutable snapshot and refreshes it\nonly after controlled active-theme edits or replacement commits. The unrestricted\nmutable active-theme accessor was removed; duplicate, whitespace-ambiguous, and\nempty available-theme identities are rejected before registration, while graph\nregistration reports the rejection and continues safely.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-theme` passed\n(21 tests); `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\nfocused shell suite remains blocked by the pre-existing\n`InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`.\n\n## 2026-08-22 — Publish the authoritative rendered theme snapshot\n\n`working tree` · area: theme snapshot state and events\n\nThe shell now publishes the rendered theme snapshot as the sole `mesh.theme`\nstate source and declares shell-owned `ThemeChanged` and `TokenChanged`\ninterface events. Theme changes carry mode, color scheme, contrast, revision,\nthe complete effective token/provenance maps, and deterministic changed-token\nrecords; individual token events carry the same revision and winning-layer\nprovenance. Provider state cannot overwrite the rendered snapshot.\n\n**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-theme` passed\n(20 tests); `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\nfocused shell suite is blocked by a pre-existing compile error in\n`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229` (`InterfaceProvider`\ncannot be dereferenced).\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Retain last-known-good theme candidates\n\n`4d11e431` · area: theme/profile/reload transactions\n\nProfile switches prepare graph-selected theme sources before committing the\ncandidate runtime. Reload parsing and component invalidation failures now keep\nthe prior theme/watch snapshot and record `theme_reload_rejected`; successful\nprofile theme changes preserve the existing catalog while replacing only the\nprepared active snapshot.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\nnetwork access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Open theme selections through contained graph sources\n\n`73dcc5ad` · area: theme source security and selection\n\nTheme selection now accepts only graph-scoped catalog identities. Their mode\nsources open beneath the owning module root with no-follow directory/file\nhandles, bounded reads, regular-file checks, and UTF-8 validation; registration\nand reload use the same boundary. Legacy free-form selection paths are rejected\nby the shell control path.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\nnetwork access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Build the graph-authorized theme catalog\n\n`447d2b43` · area: theme catalog and module graph\n\nEnabled canonical theme contributions now form one deterministic catalog keyed\nby owner-scoped identity. Descriptors retain owner module, label, ordered modes,\nvalidated default mode, and a module-root-relative source handle. Runtime theme\nregistration consumes that catalog instead of scanning `config/themes`.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\nnetwork access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Commit signed module provenance verification\n\n`fcd5fd47` · area: module provenance and candidate planning\n\nThe detached `module.sig` verifier, root-graph trust anchors, lock/graph\nprovenance checks, installer/update enforcement, and specification updates are\nnow committed. The implementation fails closed for unknown keys, malformed or\nchanged signatures, unsupported algorithms, and unverifiable verified tiers.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\nnetwork access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Close the unified service call transaction improvement\n\n`working tree` · area: service call lifecycle and optimistic state\n\nThe existing call path now covers the full improvement: Luau awaitable tickets\npublish one correlated CallId, shell routing preserves it, backend controls\nenforce deadlines and cancellation, coalescing produces explicit superseded\noutcomes, debug/profiling records retain the ID, and optimistic state writes\nsettle or roll back against that same transaction. Terminal delivery is\nidempotent and stale provider generations are rejected.\n\nVerification: source inspection confirms the shared CallId path; `cargo fmt\n--all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused Cargo checks could not\nstart because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Separate consumer and provider compatibility classes\n\n`working tree` · area: service contract update compatibility\n\nBidirectional contract diffs now expose an independent\n`CompatibilityClassification` containing the consumer and provider result\nclasses. The classification preserves asymmetric changes such as additive state\nfor consumers that is breaking for providers, while retaining each direction's\nfull change list for diagnostics.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo checks could not start because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network\naccess is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Negotiate service feature groups per provider\n\n`working tree` · area: service contract capability and provider selection\n\nContracts now compile explicit `capabilities.featureGroups` entries with\nrequired/optional semantics and normalized capability lists. The interface\nregistry and immutable catalog track provider feature advertisements per\nprovider, enable matching groups, tolerate unavailable optional groups, and\nreject providers missing required groups. Lookup diagnostics explain required\nfeature-group gaps, and compatibility diffs classify optional additions as\nadditive and required additions as breaking.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo checks could not start because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network\naccess is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Generate service contract authoring artifacts\n\n`working tree` · area: contract tooling and Luau authoring\n\nThe compiled contract now generates deterministic Luau consumer type modules,\nprovider skeletons with explicit `start(self)`, in-memory mocks with call\nrecording, and standalone Markdown documentation. All four outputs consume the\nsame normalized schemas, operation policy, behavioral metadata, fingerprints,\nand provenance; generation returns strings and does not write files.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo checks could not start because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network\naccess is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Publish immutable compiled service contracts\n\n`working tree` · area: service contract compilation and catalog authority\n\nService contracts now compile into an immutable, shared `CompiledContract`\nartifact. It owns canonical identity, normalized recursive schemas, unique\ndeclaration symbols, operation capability policy, behavioral transport metadata,\nstable schema/policy/behavior fingerprints, and declaration provenance. Mutable\nregistration and immutable catalog snapshots publish the same `Arc` artifact;\nresolutions retain a raw compatibility view for callers still migrating.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo checks could not start because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network\naccess is unavailable.\n\n--- locale mentions in status ---\n8-## Now\n9-\n10:Theme availability now comes from the enabled installed graph: canonical theme\n11-contributions produce owner-scoped descriptors with validated modes/defaults and\n12-module-root-relative source handles, and runtime registration no longer scans\n13:`config/themes`. Theme selection and reload now use graph identities and\n14:symlink-safe, bounded UTF-8 source opening. Transactional theme/profile commits\n15:now prepare candidates before profile commit and retain the last-known-good\n16:snapshot after reload failures. The active snapshot now composes recovery\n17:defaults, the selected graph pack/mode, module-owned frontend layers, and sparse\n18-user token overrides with per-value provenance. Theme selection is durable and\n19:publishes its selected mode, source fingerprint, and snapshot revision; reload\n20-polling follows content fingerprints even after a source directory changes.\n21-Component and theme CSS now lower through one restricted selector AST; theme\n--\n23-theme CSS is rejected with bounded declaration parsing. Theme and component\n24-keyframes now share runtime lowering, token aliases resolve transitively with\n25:cycle diagnostics, and computed style snapshots carry inherited custom\n26:properties. The rendered theme snapshot is authoritative for the shell's\n27-mode, color scheme, contrast, effective tokens, provenance, and revisioned\n28-`ThemeChanged`/`TokenChanged` interface events; provider mirrors cannot replace\n29:it. ThemeEngine now retains immutable active snapshot identity and rejects\n30-ambiguous registrations. Explicit manual, system-following, and scheduled mode\n31-policies now use the same transaction path; policy changes are persisted in the\n32:theme snapshot and trigger the same revisioned event/invalidation flow. Locale\n33:catalogs now expose explicit\n34-module/core translators: templates, Luau, manifests, debug metadata, and\n35-generated component settings use the owning module without global key leakage.\n36:Locale selection is now normalized and immutable at the snapshot boundary:\n37-BCP 47 tags derive parent and terminal fallback chains, direction and revision\n38:are published with the locale service state, and invalid runtime tags are\n39:rejected. Typed catalog compilation now preserves valid siblings, validates\n40-placeholder parity, and resolves bounded plural/select entries in Rust and\n41:Luau. LocaleEngine now publishes revisioned immutable catalog Arcs with an\n42:explicit replacement seam; the remaining snapshot work is wiring that seam\n43:through graph/profile/reload preparation and last-known-good commits. Surface\n44:mounts now prepare all graph catalogs into a candidate and commit one catalog\n45-Arc only when asset reads succeed.\n46-\n47-The first four Section 2 items are complete. Package commits publish\n48-validated module trees into a read-only SHA-256 object store and activate\n49:generation-stamped snapshots. Installed-graph and shell discovery resolve the\n50-active object set, while editable module trees remain the authoring/update\n51:source. Shell runtime instances and catalogs now come only from the validated\n52:resolved graph, and invalid startup/reload candidates fail closed. Candidate\n53:updates explain normalized graph changes before commit, while frontend primary\n54-entries and extension-point contributions are indexed incrementally with\n55-module-scoped source diagnostics and retryable watch paths. Focused package and\n56:catalog tests are added; Cargo\n57-execution is currently blocked\n58-because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\n--\n61-entries carry local/community/core/verified classification and detached\n62-signature metadata, `module.sig` signs a canonical module identity/version/\n63:digest payload, and root-graph `trustPolicy.keys` verify Ed25519 signatures\n64:before install, staged update review, lock verification, or graph activation.\n65-Invalid signatures and unknown keys fail closed with scoped diagnostics;\n66-registry key distribution remains a future integration.\n--\n75-\n76-Correlated service calls now complete end to end. Luau interface methods return\n77:cooperative tickets with `poll`/`await` snapshots and caller-driven\n78-cancellation; call IDs and originating instance identities survive shell\n79-dispatch, throttling, backend coalescing, provider replacement, and terminal\n--\n83-Optimistic contract-bound service writes are now CallId- and provider-generation\n84-scoped transactions. Successful calls retain their overlay until a matching\n85:provider snapshot confirms the value; failed, timed-out, cancelled, superseded,\n86-unavailable, and replaced-provider calls roll back to the prior visible state.\n87-Older completions cannot roll back a newer write, and chained failures unwind to\n--\n98-\n99-Service registration now serializes contract/provider mutations through one\n100:lock-protected snapshot and publishes generation-stamped `ResolvedServiceCatalog`\n101:views. Registry resolution, runtime catalog creation, frontend binding,\n102:profile startup, and debug inspection consume one coherent contract/provider\n103:epoch; an already-published catalog remains unchanged when later registrations\n104-arrive.\n105-\n106-The transitional untyped `ServiceRegistry` has been removed. Shell service\n107:authority now lives only in the generation-stamped interface catalog; no\n108-`TypeId`/`Any` backend store remains.\n109-\n--\n121-Contracts now declare `capabilities.featureGroups` with explicit optional or\n122-required semantics. Provider feature advertisements are normalized per provider\n123:in the generation-stamped catalog: optional groups degrade without rejecting the\n124-provider, while missing required groups make the provider unavailable and are\n125-reported in interface lookup diagnostics.\n--\n137-Service contracts now validate recursive named fields, arrays, optional values,\n138-and `Result` returns across state, events, method inputs, and provider outputs.\n139:Invalid snapshots preserve the last-known-good state, invalid events and\n140-commands are rejected at their boundaries, and provider result violations settle\n141-as contract failures. Luau method proxies enforce arity and argument types with\n--\n144-\n145-Provider runtimes now prepare privately and activate only after lifecycle\n146:readiness plus a valid initial snapshot for stateful interfaces. Startup,\n147:supervised restart, live provider switch, and profile candidates all buffer\n148-prepared output and replay it once after commit; invalid preparation leaves the\n149-current provider and state in place. Health transitions are published after the\n--\n163-network access; formatting and whitespace checks pass.\n164-\n165:Contract identity is now canonicalized when compiled and at every graph,\n166-runtime, diagnostics, update, and LSP index boundary. Short aliases resolve to\n167-the same `InterfaceContract` artifact as canonical names, including provider\n--\n177-\n178-On NetworkManager hosts, live Codex network-wait errors trigger a throttled\n179:attempt to restore the most recently used saved Wi-Fi profile; set\n180-`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0` to disable it.\n181-\n--\n186-Package install, update, uninstall, and rollback now share the core\n187-`PackageTransaction` engine: one OS lock, a durable journal, staged candidates,\n188:filesystem snapshots, and crash recovery. The completed backlog item and its\n189-validation record are in the 2026-08 log.\n190-\n191:Update planning now materializes every candidate and unchanged graph member in\n192:the transaction workspace, resolves external interface contracts and profile\n193:closure from that staged graph, and records approval-filtered capability\n194-decisions before any live checkout or replacement.\n195-\n--\n200-\n201-Section 01 improvement 1 is complete: module capability declarations now resolve\n202:against a closed catalog and persisted root-graph approvals before activation;\n203-required grants fail without approval, optional grants default to denied, and\n204-frontend/backend runtimes receive only the resolved grant set.\n--\n206-Section 01 improvement 2 is complete: shell operations now pass through one\n207-typed registry that validates payloads, required capabilities, and caller\n208:constraints. Raw shell events, locale writes, and popover helpers share that\n209-authorization path; malformed, unauthorized, unknown, and dropped requests\n210-produce structured diagnostics.\n--\n219-resolved state. Health aggregates active issues without severity downgrades;\n220-collector registration replaces identities, unregister removes stale instance\n221:state, snapshots are deterministic, and backend recovery resolves lifecycle\n222-issues explicitly.\n223-\n--\n230-now derive the versioned telemetry wire shape, including the invalidation fields\n231-that had drifted out of the shell payload. The shell adds the schema version and\n232:keeps only overlay/controller state plus the established module-graph compatibility\n233-adapter outside the DTO boundary.\n234-\n--\n237-root. I/O and irrecoverable JSON parse failures remain fatal.\n238-\n239:Module settings namespaces now receive complete owner-registered schema snapshots\n240:from the installed graph. Registration and graph replacement stage validation\n241-before commit; invalid values are omitted from the runtime projection while raw\n242-unknown or uninstalled namespaces remain available for lifecycle repair.\n--\n262-Module package boundaries now validate typed `@scope/name` identities and\n263-contained relative paths before filesystem use. Installed trees, manifests,\n264:locks, profiles, graph entries, installers, updates, and removals reject\n265-symlinks; recursive discovery, source scanning, and digest traversal use\n266-visited canonical directories and never follow links outside their roots.\n--\n292-The Section 16 developer and authoring tools audit is complete. Its end-to-end\n293-CLI/LSP process tree, package-transaction and path-safety findings, canonical\n294:authoring-snapshot direction, LSP protocol findings, and regression matrix are\n295-in [`sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md`](log/sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md);\n296-the resulting open work is in `docs/BACKLOG.md`.\n--\n305-The Nix development shell now recommends the optimized release shell command,\n306-keeps the debug command explicit for debugging, and the shell logs its Cargo\n307:build profile at startup. Production paints no longer build the focused proof\n308:snapshot; the proof remains available to the shell regression tests.\n309-\n310-Animation-only frames now reuse the targeted restyle decision and skip an\n--\n313-repainting instead of forcing a full tree rebuild.\n314-\n315:Animation transition state and previous visual snapshots now key on stable\n316-`NodeId`s. The transition pass no longer allocates a mesh-key string and then\n317-clones it into a live set for every node; authored keyframe names remain\n--\n364-\n365-The always-on navigation bar now has a frame-cost baseline of its own\n366:(`navigation_frame_cost_profile`), alongside the existing Appearance profile.\n367-Settled at 1920x56 with 107 nodes it costs 0.41ms for a plain repaint, 1.36ms\n368-for a pointer move, 1.60ms for a service poll nothing on the bar reads, and\n--\n379-service proxy, the service state member, nor `last_service_update` now takes\n380-the value non-reactively. Declaring `render()` no longer disables the selective\n381:service build either — the hooks run before the frame snapshots its dirty\n382-flags, and the gate is what they actually wrote. A Settings Appearance frame\n383-driven by an unrelated audio poll went from 12.2–12.4ms to 8.2–8.6ms.\n--\n385-The existing settings device page now has a typed `mesh.device` provider. The\n386-new `@mesh/device-info` Linux backend reports OS identity, kernel and\n387:architecture, desktop session, CPU, memory, graphics, hostname, and uptime\n388:through the generic backend runtime; optional `lspci` support enriches graphics\n389-details without making the provider unavailable.\n390-\n391-Author-declared visual customization is now live as a module-system feature.\n392-Named `mode=\"customizable\"` slots select ordered public component placements\n393:from profile schema 3, while ordinary slots keep automatic extension-point\n394-behavior. Core provides generic discovery, validation, optimistic generation\n395-checks, sparse persistence, and mounting through `mesh.composition`; it owns no\n--\n420-VM as an in-tree promoted popover. Core slider capture keeps drag updates alive\n421-while the pointer crosses the popup boundary; no standalone audio surface is\n422:enabled by the shipped graph.\n423-\n424-Promoted legacy popovers now measure their first frame against the known parent\n--\n436-Pure paint-only scroll frames now reuse computed styles and retained layout, so\n437-large Appearance resource lists do not rerun the full style walk on every tick.\n438:The Appearance scroll regression covers the bounded icon/font catalog path.\n439-\n440-The Phase26 real-surface proof is deterministic under the default parallel\n--\n447-all-runtime descendant scan while preserving nested-state invalidation.\n448-\n449:Retained layout now receives sparse owned snapshots for layout-dirty nodes and\n450-updates their Taffy styles/text contexts directly. The persistent Taffy mapping\n451-and structural/unkeyed reconciliation fallback are now authoritative across\n--\n453-\n454-The typed `mesh.packages` provider now owns module installation and removal as\n455:well as provider selection, enablement, and profile switching. Local and Git\n456:sources update the installed graph, lock, and profile state through the same\n457-core path used by the CLI.\n458-\n--\n471-  --check`: passed.\n472-\n473:- Node-slot parser/compiler, profile merge, module graph, and request mapping:\n474-  passed.\n475-- Shipped frontend compilation, navigation layout/raster/hover/keyboard/pointer\n--\n501-- `real_surfaces` slice: 44 passed, 3 failed, 3 ignored. The three failures are\n502-  the already-recorded shipped-navigation layout and inspector fixture\n503:  baseline; both frame-cost profiles are `#[ignore]` and did not run in that\n504-  suite, so they cannot have caused them.\n505-- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
