{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' .planning/README.md && printf '\\\\n--- backlog headings/items ---\\\\n' && rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|''^- '\"\\\\[ \\\\]' docs/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"# MESH planning\n\nProgress tracking for MESH. Plain Markdown, updated by hand, no tooling — if a\nfile here needs a program to stay correct, it is the wrong shape.\n\nThis directory answers three questions, and each has exactly one home:\n\n| Question | File | Shape |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What am I doing right now? | [`STATUS.md`](STATUS.md) | One page. Overwritten as work moves. |\n| What is still open? | [`../docs/BACKLOG.md`](../docs/BACKLOG.md) | Terse checklist. Items leave when done. |\n| What happened, and what did it measure? | [`log/`](log/) | Append-only, dated. Never edited after the fact. |\n\nThe split exists because the old backlog tried to be all three at once and grew\nto 1,250 lines, with individual items carrying hundred-line progress narratives.\nA backlog that records history stops being scannable, and history that lives\ninside a checklist gets silently rewritten.\n\n## The rules\n\n1. **The backlog says what is open, not what happened.** An item is one to three\n   lines: what to do, and why it is not done. No dated progress paragraphs, no\n   benchmark numbers. If an item needs detail, link to a log entry.\n2. **Finishing an item removes it from the backlog.** Its record goes to the\n   log. `[x]` entries do not accumulate in `docs/BACKLOG.md`; a completed item\n   that stays visible is just history in the wrong file.\n3. **The log is append-only.** Correct a wrong entry with a new dated entry that\n   says so. Never revise a past one — the numbers in it are the baseline\n   something later gets compared against.\n4. **Every performance claim carries its measurement.** Machine, build profile,\n   workload shape, before and after, and the gate name if one exists. A speedup\n   without its workload cannot be reproduced or trusted.\n5. **Record what failed.** An approach that was measured and reverted is worth\n   more than one that was never tried, because it stops the next person\n   repeating it. See the rejected-experiments table in\n   [`log/performance-log.md`](log/performance-log.md).\n6. **`STATUS.md` is disposable.** It describes the present. Overwrite it freely;\n   the log is what remembers.\n\n## Layout\n\n```\n.planning/\n  README.md      this file — how the system works\n  STATUS.md      current position: in flight, next, blocked\n  log/           dated history; append-only\n  archive/       frozen; superseded or tool-era records\n  todos/         standalone plans too big for a backlog line\n  codebase/      structural analysis of the source tree\n  research/      external investigation feeding decisions\n  spikes/        throwaway experiments and their conclusions\n  prototypes/    working code kept for reference\n  debug/         recorded debugging investigations\n  notes/         everything that outlived its conversation\n  frontend/  renderer/  seeds/\n```\n\n### `log/`\n\nDated history. See [`log/README.md`](log/README.md) for what goes where and the\nentry format. Current files:\n\n- `2026-07.md` — the running monthly work log.\n- `performance-log.md` — performance history and the rejected-experiments\n  table, back to 2026-07-02.\n- `backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md` — verbatim snapshot of the pre-split backlog,\n  holding the detailed progress narratives for items still open.\n- `sections.md` — the current package-oriented concern map; section letters\n  (A–V) in older records are historical references.\n\n### `archive/`\n\nFrozen. Nothing here is maintained and nothing here is authoritative — it is\nkept because it explains how the project arrived where it is. See\n[`archive/README.md`](archive/README.md).\n\n### `todos/`\n\nA backlog line that needs a design before it can be executed gets a file in\n`todos/pending/`, and the backlog line links to it. Moves to `todos/completed/`\nwhen the work lands, with the outcome appended.\n\n## Relationship to `docs/`\n\n`docs/` is the contract: [`docs/spec/`](../docs/spec/) defines behavior and\nmarks each section `Shipped` or `Target`; the guides describe how things\ncurrently work. `.planning/` is evidence and intent. When the two disagree about\nwhat MESH *does*, the spec wins — a planning document may explain why a decision\nwas made, but it never overrides the specification.\n\n--- backlog headings/items ---\n24:## Shell features\n26:## Section audit feature checklist\n32:### 1. Core foundation contracts\n38:### 2. Module system and installation\n43:### 3. Service contracts\n48:### 4. Themes\n52:### 5. Localization and i18n\n56:- [ ] Thread the immutable locale catalog snapshot through atomic graph/profile/selection/reload commits with last-known-good recovery.\n57:- [ ] Add targeted ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance, and duplicate precedence checks.\n58:- [ ] Route locale changes through one durable revision-checked settings/profile transaction.\n59:- [ ] Align `mesh.i18n`, `mesh.locale`, runtime/LSP ABI, and read/write capability enforcement.\n60:- [ ] Centralize localized metadata and visible miss handling with owner, fallback, source, and snapshot revision.\n61:- [ ] Open bounded catalogs through contained no-follow source handles and compile them off the shell thread.\n62:- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/extraction/doctor tooling from the canonical graph, and implement or explicitly reject `<i18n>` blocks.\n63:- [ ] Add manual, follow-system, and per-profile locale policies through the same prepare/persist/commit path.\n65:### 6. Host resources and icon packs\n69:- [ ] Build icon and font resources only from the prepared graph/profile and atomically retain the last-known-good resource snapshot.\n70:- [ ] Preserve declared pack order and reject duplicate canonical IDs, aliases, and ambiguous ownership deterministically.\n71:- [ ] Open module resource assets through contained, no-follow, bounded handles.\n72:- [ ] Implement canonical semantic and dash-generalized icon fallback resolution with required/optional diagnostics.\n73:- [ ] Preserve typed multicolor mappings, source color policy, and mapping provenance.\n74:- [ ] Validate complete packs off the render thread and publish them atomically without partial registry state.\n75:- [ ] Propagate resource revisions/fingerprints through negative lookup, glyph, font, text-layout, and renderer caches.\n76:- [ ] Implement font-pack runtime resolution with logical roles, pack-qualified roles, faces, coverage, and typography tokens.\n77:- [ ] Unify XDG icon/font discovery and lookup into one ordered, refreshable host catalog.\n78:- [ ] Prepare resource parsing and asset handles away from shell/render threads with bounded cancellation.\n79:- [ ] Generate runtime, CLI, LSP, doctor, and debug resource explanations from one effective snapshot.\n80:- [ ] Add a resource coverage advisor for semantic vocabulary and font-script gaps without silently reordering user chains.\n82:### 7. Component language\n86:- [ ] Make local component imports module-relative, canonicalized, contained, and symlink-safe across compiler, watcher, and LSP paths.\n87:- [ ] Resolve recursive imports by owner scope and canonical target, with collision and cycle diagnostics.\n88:- [ ] Replace lossy block extraction with a span-preserving top-level parser that validates required, unique, attributed blocks and `<i18n>` policy.\n89:- [ ] Parse interpolations and control-flow braces with a real lexer/parser that rejects malformed expressions and preserves spans.\n90:- [ ] Add semantic validation linking `<props>`, `prop()` references, child props, visibility, types, and CSS domains.\n91:- [ ] Select the highest valid value across prop/configuration layers while retaining invalid overrides only in diagnostics.\n92:- [ ] Make JSON-to-prop conversion scalar-only until an explicit structured prop type exists.\n93:- [ ] Normalize and validate `PropDef` constraints, units, options, tokens, and CSS-domain values once.\n94:- [ ] Replace line-oriented script/import/symbol scans with one Luau lexer/parser and source metadata.\n95:- [ ] Carry reliable source spans through component AST nodes, compiler errors, CLI diagnostics, and LSP output.\n97:### 8. UI element core\n101:- [ ] Normalize roles, names, descriptions, hidden state, ARIA aliases, focus, relationships, and visibility after child construction and publish the semantic snapshot.\n102:- [ ] Use visible descendant text in accessible-name precedence and preserve hidden-child and locale behavior.\n103:- [ ] Generate one typed pseudo-state table for indexing, mutation, matching, invalidation, diagnostics, and tests.\n104:- [ ] Make compiler inheritance matching consume the actual element state.\n105:- [ ] Make retained layout transactional, preserve last-known-good geometry, and retry after failure.\n106:- [ ] Use one stateful input dispatcher with pointer capture, press-origin identity, activation semantics, focus eligibility, and invalidation output.\n107:- [ ] Generate element types, source/runtime tags, contracts, events, attributes, style hooks, and accessibility defaults from one schema.\n108:- [ ] Detect CSS custom-property cycles with structured diagnostics and specified invalid-value fallback.\n109:- [ ] Preserve explicit/inherited property masks through retained style resolution and targeted restyle.\n110:- [ ] Derive accessibility focus from canonical live focus state during snapshot generation.\n111:- [ ] Include all shaping inputs and resource/measurer revisions in text measurement contexts and cache keys.\n112:- [ ] Make popover placement tokens and trigger/surface relationships typed, validated, and observable across promotion.\n113:- [ ] Introduce an immutable frame snapshot with phase stamps, semantic diffs, stable identities, and property-based invariant tests.\n115:### 9. Interaction and motion\n119:- [ ] Share visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and target filtering across interaction, rendering, focus, scrolling, tooltips, and accessibility.\n120:- [ ] Use one affine transform/clip contract for hit testing, paint bounds, scrolling, and focus geometry.\n121:- [ ] Prevent disabled and inert nodes, including descendants and captured targets, from receiving activation.\n122:- [ ] Preserve keyframe progress across pause/resume and iteration-boundary changes.\n123:- [ ] Add a `MotionPolicy` snapshot for reduced motion across transitions, keyframes, scrolling, inertia, tooltips, and surfaces.\n124:- [ ] Consolidate focus, pointer capture, press origin, gesture ownership, and scroll ownership into one transaction.\n125:- [ ] Preserve the previous discrete value during visibility transitions until the transition completes.\n126:- [ ] Propagate validated per-keyframe easing through component parsing, shell state, and animation sampling.\n127:- [ ] Keep interaction policy in a renderer-neutral frame/state-machine contract with typed decisions and dirty outputs.\n128:- [ ] Implement `box-shadow` parsing with structured errors or reject it before the public animation API.\n129:- [ ] Give animation instances stable identity and explicit replacement, cancellation, and reversal semantics.\n130:- [ ] Introduce an `InteractionFrame` shared by input, state, style invalidation, layout, animation, paint, and semantics.\n132:### 10. Frontend compiler and host\n136:- [ ] Reject absolute, traversal, and symlinked frontend entrypoint/import paths outside the module root.\n137:- [ ] Keep service payloads Rust-owned unless the consumer has the resolved read capability; event subscriptions alone must not expose state.\n138:- [ ] Reload primary and contribution roots together as one atomic catalog generation.\n139:- [ ] Validate contribution roots against interface requirements, availability, and version ranges.\n140:- [ ] Validate root and nested expression scopes uniformly using parser/runtime symbol tables and source spans.\n141:- [ ] Compile expressions once into shared semantics so preview, live, translation, and composition paths behave identically.\n142:- [ ] Split the frontend host into a renderer-neutral ABI and shell adapters with typed capability-scoped effects.\n143:- [ ] Build one reverse dependency graph over primary and contribution roots for invalidation.\n144:- [ ] Scope local component aliases by owner and canonical source, rejecting collisions.\n145:- [ ] Replace line-oriented Luau symbol discovery with parser/compiler metadata or remove it as a source of truth.\n146:- [ ] Make expression scanning UTF-8 safe and diagnose non-ASCII input instead of panicking.\n147:- [ ] Publish runtime props transactionally so Rust and Luau cannot diverge after a failed update.\n148:- [ ] Preserve typed diagnostic categories and source spans through AST, compiler, shell, LSP, and debug paths.\n149:- [ ] Prevent stale catalog rollback from overwriting a newer generation using a coordinator or compare-and-swap.\n150:- [ ] Enforce distinct popover and overflow child-surface lifecycle, focus, dismissal, placement, and ownership semantics.\n151:- [ ] Dispatch frontend `mount` and `unmount` hooks in every initialization, reload, deactivation, and replacement path.\n152:- [ ] Contain expression/runtime failures with last-known-good trees or bounded error placeholders and actionable diagnostics.\n153:- [ ] Publish normalized public-prop schemas and validate imported/contribution props at the import boundary.\n154:- [ ] Reject local/module import alias collisions or resolve them through one typed import namespace.\n155:- [ ] Introduce a coherent revisioned `FrontendFrame` boundary for tree, catalog, runtime, services, invalidation, diagnostics, and effects.\n156:- [ ] Emit immutable compiled frontend revisions and authorize typed effects only when catalog/runtime revisions still match.\n158:### 11. Luau runtime and sandbox\n162:- [ ] Enforce one sandbox/resource policy for every Luau realm, including instruction, memory, output, queue, storage, and child-process budgets.\n163:- [ ] Keep service state Rust-owned, generation-aware, and capability-filtered instead of exposing shared globals.\n164:- [ ] Route every backend early return, callback failure, stop, and crash through idempotent cleanup and one terminal lifecycle record.\n165:- [ ] Make unsubscribe-safe event iteration and independent subscriber failure reporting explicit.\n166:- [ ] Give stream subprocesses stable identities, bounded queues, exit events, awaited reaping, and shutdown semantics.\n167:- [ ] Move `mesh.exec` off the async backend loop and enforce cancellation, deadlines, executable policy, and output limits.\n168:- [ ] Replace basename executable grants with canonical path/argument capability policy.\n169:- [ ] Move default runtime storage to secure durable XDG state with permissions, quotas, atomic recovery, and revisioned writers.\n170:- [ ] Make backend command dispatch typed, correlated, transactional, coalescable, generation-aware, and bounded.\n171:- [ ] Gate event and side-effect queues through one typed authorization/resource boundary before acceptance.\n172:- [ ] Represent locale as a host-owned per-context cell updated with the translation snapshot.\n173:- [ ] Treat Luau `nil` writes as explicit deletions in Rust state and dependency invalidation.\n174:- [ ] Restrict provider event publication to provider-owned handles and isolate subscriber callback failures.\n175:- [ ] Preserve one stable backend `self` and event/storage handles for each runtime generation.\n176:- [ ] Publish validated initial provider state before atomically marking the provider ready.\n177:- [ ] Compile and swap a fresh Luau environment on reload, preserving only explicit durable storage.\n178:- [ ] Stage backend top-level execution so host side effects cannot escape a failed `start(self)`.\n179:- [ ] Bound command/event ingress, JSON depth/bytes, event counts, and aggregate runtime resource budgets.\n180:- [ ] Separate effective read and control grants and enforce them through both proxy and shell paths.\n181:- [ ] Reconcile changed poll intervals after every stream callback.\n182:- [ ] Return recoverable host-installation errors instead of panicking during backend setup.\n183:- [ ] Introduce a per-module `RuntimeSession` combining realm, host, resource broker, lifecycle, state, supervisor, health, backoff, and quarantine.\n185:### 12. Rendering and paint\n189:- [ ] Make `PixelCanvasSession` storage-safe so mapped drawing surfaces cannot outlive backing allocations.\n190:- [ ] Lower asymmetric four-edge borders and four-corner radii correctly.\n191:- [ ] Use one cumulative affine transform/clip model across paint, damage, blur, descendants, and interaction.\n192:- [ ] Lower node opacity and blend mode as isolated compositing groups instead of per primitive.\n193:- [ ] Include paint-order/topology changes in display-list generations and stable equal-z ordering.\n194:- [ ] Unify dirty contracts and paint signatures for all content/style fields, including controls, text, icons, and variables.\n195:- [ ] Include font/resource revisions in text style painting and all glyph/font/text cache keys.\n196:- [ ] Make backdrop blur an explicit renderer/backend capability with validated fallback behavior.\n197:- [ ] Use one fractional-scale rounding policy across layout, buffer, paint, copy, and protocol damage.\n198:- [ ] Preserve release batch/barrier metrics in diagnostics and debug telemetry.\n199:- [ ] Validate caller lineage before trusting generation shortcuts.\n200:- [ ] Include resource revisions in retained-present decisions and cache invalidation.\n201:- [ ] Preserve pending damage and avoid acknowledging unshown frames after presentation errors.\n202:- [ ] Move resource decode and rasterization off the frame thread through a bounded resource broker.\n203:- [ ] Enforce byte- and dimension-based cache budgets for decoded assets, fonts, glyphs, text, Skia, PixelBuffer, and SHM allocations.\n204:- [ ] Introduce a typed frame paint plan with immutable inputs, topology, transforms, effect regions, replay spans, and exact logical/device damage.\n206:### 13. Surface policy and configuration\n210:- [ ] Enforce the author-only `promotable` guard for settings, IPC, and live role changes.\n211:- [ ] Remove or formally protect `promotable` from user-overridable/ejected settings.\n212:- [ ] Validate manifest surface enums and role constraints with canonical parsers before graph resolution.\n213:- [ ] Include blur and window decorations in semantic presentation change detection.\n214:- [ ] Route settings-driven role reloads through the same transactional transition supervisor as explicit promotion.\n215:- [ ] Share role-field metadata across manifest diagnostics, settings validation, ejection, and protocol lowering.\n216:- [ ] Preserve localization identity and distinguish effective values from overrides during configuration ejection.\n217:- [ ] Use typed unmeasured/content/surface/wire extents at the shell/presentation boundary.\n218:- [ ] Replace split surface field lists with a revisioned semantic policy diff and generation.\n219:- [ ] Build the policy compiler producing declared contracts, effective snapshots, and typed transition plans.\n221:### 14. Wayland platform and presentation\n225:- [ ] Return typed create/configure/present/lost outcomes, cache only accepted generations, and retain damage for missing surfaces.\n226:- [ ] Route close, dismiss, parent destruction, role replacement, and connection loss through one idempotent teardown supervisor.\n227:- [ ] Replace incomplete presentation hashes and warm region caches with typed semantic diffs and object/configure/frame generations.\n228:- [ ] Preserve click-grab seat and serial identity through input normalization, popup requests, and one-shot configuration.\n229:- [ ] Gate popup reposition by negotiated protocol version and validate popup role, parent, identity, and reparenting.\n230:- [ ] Use one authoritative logical extent for paint, buffer validation, viewport, regions, attach, and queries.\n231:- [ ] Commit opaque, blur, and other surface-state changes even when pixel damage is empty.\n232:- [ ] Separate frame-callback waiting from buffer-release backpressure and prevent hot retry loops.\n233:- [ ] Make input ownership per seat and cancel pointer, touch, gesture, focus, and repeat transactions during teardown.\n234:- [ ] Validate buffer length/stride/scale, propagate attach/region errors, expose connection loss, and reap clipboard children.\n235:- [ ] Replace the recorder backend with a deterministic protocol-state lifecycle simulator plus a focused live compositor matrix.\n236:- [ ] Add full pointer-button identity and IME/text-input-v3 support while preserving normalized Escape behavior.\n237:- [ ] Build the transactional, capability-aware presentation engine with version reporting, reactive popups, multi-output membership, per-seat input, and shared SHM/dmabuf/GPU frame contracts.\n239:### 15. Shell core and orchestration\n243:- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one immutable activation plan and atomic runtime-generation commit.\n244:- [ ] Reconcile backend enable/disable and every graph delta through the same activation coordinator.\n245:- [ ] Tag backend tasks, bridges, messages, events, results, and restart deadlines with activation generations and provider epochs.\n246:- [ ] Make frontend/backend stop, unmount, storage flush, worker join, and shutdown cleanup explicit and idempotent.\n247:- [ ] Give detached workers owned wake handles and lifecycle guards that outlive the eventfd until join.\n248:- [ ] Isolate component/runtime callback, tick, build, render, and reload failures with placeholders, diagnostics, and quarantine.\n249:- [ ] Replace static startup watching with a managed generation-aware watch set and bounded polling fallback.\n250:- [ ] Process CoreRequest effects through one fair bounded scheduler with causal budgets and cycle detection.\n251:- [ ] Publish provider unavailable/recovery transitions from committed provider generations.\n252:- [ ] Route control-plane writes through declared durable revisions and ordered settings/theme/locale effect batches.\n253:- [ ] Distinguish intentional legacy/no-profile operation from invalid configured graph/profile recovery.\n254:- [ ] Connect package journal commit/rollback to runtime activation so disk and live state share one recoverable transaction.\n255:- [ ] Introduce `ActiveSnapshot` with candidate preview in hidden surfaces and an explicit quiescing-to-stopped shutdown state machine.\n257:### 16. Developer and authoring tools\n261:- [ ] Make uninstall path-safe and reject traversal/symlink escapes before recursive deletion.\n262:- [ ] Make package mutations atomic, journaled, crash-recoverable, and failure-injection tested.\n263:- [ ] Make live profile switching use typed acknowledgements and exact-generation recovery.\n264:- [ ] Make CLI and shell use one package ownership and transaction contract.\n265:- [ ] Refresh one canonical graph-authoring snapshot for CLI, doctor, LSP, and runtime consumers.\n266:- [ ] Generate LSP manifest/schema validation from runtime contracts instead of duplicated under-approximations.\n267:- [ ] Convert all LSP positions to UTF-16 code units at the protocol boundary.\n268:- [ ] Make `--replace` and CLI flags enforce their documented typed behavior.\n269:- [ ] Make LSP analysis syntax-aware with recoverable partial ASTs and useful mid-edit diagnostics.\n270:- [ ] Replace heuristic Luau/JSON scanners with standards-compliant parsers and source-span data.\n271:- [ ] Support workspace-folder-only initialization and versioned `didChange` refresh generations.\n272:- [ ] Select manifest flavors from parsed structure, not textual substring checks.\n273:- [ ] Make hover consume the current registry and expose service field/command documentation.\n274:- [ ] Restrict definition resolution to existing module-contained imports.\n275:- [ ] Preserve Unicode JSON escapes, registry generations, and secure import provenance in authoring diagnostics.\n277:## Foundation contracts\n279:## Module system\n285:- [ ] Close module filesystem escapes before any package mutation or source\n288:- [ ] Make installed-graph activation fail closed and build catalogs/provider\n291:- [ ] Replace the duplicate CLI/shell package implementations with one locked,\n294:- [ ] Enforce required/optional module dependencies, module/interface version\n297:- [ ] Make the canonical `module.json` loader the only production path; legacy\n300:- [ ] Make module lifecycle and health authoritative across graph, frontend, and\n303:- [ ] Align the lock schema with the specification and populate dependency and\n306:- [ ] Move the remaining built-in debug and theme/locale service behavior\n310:- [ ] **Deferred — unify the four contribution schemas.** Theme, icons, i18n,\n317:## Service contracts\n319:- [ ] Replace the additive interface registry with one immutable, atomic\n322:- [ ] Compile and enforce complete contracts end to end: canonical unique names,\n325:- [ ] Make service methods correlated request/response transactions with typed\n328:- [ ] Gate provider/profile commits on validated readiness and buffered initial\n331:- [ ] Diff the complete compiled contract in consumer and provider directions,\n335:## Themes\n337:- [ ] Make graph-authorized theme descriptors the only catalog/loader, with\n340:- [ ] Make theme selection, profile switching, graph changes, and hot reload one\n343:- [ ] Share the restricted CSS/token/keyframe lowering path between themes and\n346:- [ ] Complete `mesh.theme` and settings with modes, sparse token overrides,\n350:## Localization / i18n\n352:- [ ] Replace per-surface locale engines and the cross-module global key pool\n355:- [ ] Make locale selection a durable revisioned settings/profile transaction:\n358:- [ ] Compile bounded typed catalogs with per-entry diagnostics, interpolation,\n361:- [ ] Route templates, Luau, manifests, props, generated settings, and debug data\n364:- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/doctor, extraction, missing-key and provenance tooling\n368:## Host resources and icon packs\n370:- [ ] Replace discovery-time global resource registration with one immutable,\n373:- [ ] Make icon resolution deterministic and complete: canonical owner-scoped\n376:- [ ] Complete font-pack runtime support with logical and pack-qualified roles,\n379:- [ ] Unify XDG and font discovery into a refreshable host catalog; propagate\n382:- [ ] Remove legacy icon config and discovery authorities, and drive bounded\n386:## Component language\n388:- [ ] Replace lossy `.mesh` block extraction and ad-hoc template preprocessing\n391:- [ ] Make component compilation resolve imports per owner, enforce module-root\n394:- [ ] Make the `<props>` contract one normalized typed value pipeline: reject\n398:## UI element core\n400:- [ ] Establish unique `NodeId` tree identity and reject duplicate/unknown\n402:- [ ] Generate element contracts, runtime types, attributes, events, and\n404:- [ ] Consolidate interaction, style invalidation, retained layout, and\n407:- [ ] Normalize ARIA/visibility/name semantics after child construction and\n410:## Interaction and motion\n412:- [ ] Unify visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and\n415:- [ ] Define a canonical interaction transaction for focus, pointer capture,\n418:- [ ] Complete motion semantics: pause/resume, stable animation identity,\n421:- [ ] Implement or safely gate the public `box-shadow` parser and add the\n424:## Frontend compiler and host\n426:- [ ] Compile, validate, watch, invalidate, and reload primary and\n429:- [ ] Gate service payload publication on capabilities and replace the\n432:- [ ] Complete frontend runtime lifecycle and recovery: dispatch mount/unmount,\n435:- [ ] Unify template expression semantics and root-scope validation around the\n438:- [ ] Split the renderer/Wayland/package/debug policy out of the\n441:## Shell core and orchestration\n443:- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one revisioned activation\n447:- [ ] Reconcile every live graph delta, including backend module enable/disable;\n450:- [ ] Add explicit frontend unmount and graceful backend stop/join, then route\n453:- [ ] Replace the static startup watcher with a healthy, generation-aware watch\n456:- [ ] Centralize CoreRequest effects in one fair bounded scheduler and isolate\n459:- [ ] Make shell control-plane propagation coherent: publish provider\n463:## Developer and authoring tools\n465:- [ ] Move CLI install/update/rollback/uninstall/profile mutations behind one\n468:- [ ] Derive and refresh one canonical graph-authoring snapshot for CLI, doctor,\n471:- [ ] Make LSP parsing and protocol boundaries correct and syntax-aware:\n476:## Settings\n482:## Popovers\n489:## Performance\n499:### Render pipeline\n501:- [ ] **The narrow service path never engages for real modules.** It requires a\n509:- [ ] A root-level `backdrop-filter` collapses all partial damage to the whole\n514:- [ ] Continue widening generation shortcuts to per-node dirty scoping and\n517:- [ ] Display-list segment/rope command storage → v1.21. Command arrays are\n521:- [ ] Close the Section 12 correctness gaps before further paint optimization:\n526:### Style\n528:- [ ] Typed style declarations end-to-end: resolve theme tokens to typed values\n533:- [ ] Interaction frames still re-apply string style declarations per node —\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
