{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' .codex-continuation.md && sed -n '1,240p' docs/architecture/overview.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/spec/README.md && sed -n '1,260p' docs/BACKLOG.md && sed -n '1,220p' .planning/STATUS.md && sed -n '1,220p' .planning/log/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' .codex-continuation.md && sed -n '1,240p' docs/architecture/overview.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/spec/README.md && sed -n '1,260p' docs/BACKLOG.md && sed -n '1,220p' .planning/STATUS.md && sed -n '1,220p' .planning/log/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"# MESH continuation handoff\n\n## Objective\n\nWork through `docs/BACKLOG.md` from highest priority downward. Implement one\ncoherent increment at a time, verify it, remove completed items from the\nbacklog, and continue autonomously. The next highest-priority unfinished item\nis:\n\n`Add targeted ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance, and duplicate precedence checks.`\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Read and follow `docs/architecture/overview.md`, `docs/spec/README.md`, and\n  `.planning/README.md` before changing code.\n- `docs/BACKLOG.md` is the only open-work list. Keep `STATUS.md` short and logs\n  append-only; completed backlog items leave the file.\n- Avoid unrelated changes. Preserve the pre-existing dirty `codex-loop.log`.\n- Use the Nix dev shell for Cargo commands and `apply_patch` for edits.\n- Services remain Luau modules; service-specific behavior must not move into\n  Rust shell code.\n- `.planning/archive/` is frozen and not authoritative.\n\n## Completed work\n\n- Theme snapshots are shell-owned authoritative rendered state with immutable\n  identity, complete tokens/provenance, revisioned `ThemeChanged` and\n  `TokenChanged` events, deterministic registration, and manual,\n  follow-system, and scheduled mode policies through transactional paths.\n- Localization now has explicit core/module translators, normalized immutable\n  BCP 47 locale selections with parent chains, direction, and revisions, plus\n  bounded typed text/plural/select catalog compilation with diagnostics and\n  interpolation.\n- `LocaleEngine` stores catalogs in immutable revisioned `Arc` snapshots and\n  can prepare complete replacement candidates without mutating the active\n  snapshot.\n- Graph discovery/cached loading, module enable/disable, provider selection,\n  backend replacement, profile switching, and settings locale reloads prepare\n  catalog candidates before committing graph/profile/selection/runtime state.\n  Read or parse failures retain the last-known-good state.\n- Shell-created components consume the coordinator’s catalog `Arc`; standalone\n  component fixtures retain their source-backed compatibility path.\n- The locale catalog transaction item was removed from `docs/BACKLOG.md`; the\n  result is recorded in `.planning/log/2026-08.md` and `.planning/STATUS.md`.\n\n## Repository and git state\n\n- Repository: `/home/kolby/projects/mesh`.\n- All work is uncommitted; no commit was created.\n- `codex-loop.log` was already modified by automation and must remain untouched.\n- `Cargo.lock` contains the intended theme dependency changes from prior work;\n  do not regenerate unrelated lockfile content.\n- The working tree contains the combined prior theme/localization work and the\n  current locale transaction increment. Preserve unrelated existing changes.\n\n## Files changed\n\n- Tracking/handoff: `.codex-continuation.md`, `.planning/STATUS.md`,\n  `.planning/log/2026-08.md`, `docs/BACKLOG.md`, `Cargo.lock`.\n- Foundation: `crates/core/foundation/config/Cargo.toml`,\n  `crates/core/foundation/config/src/lib.rs`,\n  `crates/core/foundation/locale/src/lib.rs`,\n  `crates/core/foundation/theme/src/lib.rs`.\n- Scripting: files under\n  `crates/core/runtime/scripting/src/context/` and `state.rs`.\n- Shell orchestration: `crates/core/shell/src/shell/{mod.rs,discovery.rs,\n  profile.rs,package.rs,surface_layout.rs}` plus runtime files under\n  `crates/core/shell/src/shell/runtime/` and backend lifecycle code.\n- Shell components: files under\n  `crates/core/shell/src/shell/component/`.\n- Shell tests: existing theme/debug/keybind updates and the new graph locale\n  replacement/recovery regression in `crates/core/shell/src/shell/tests/graph.rs`.\n\n## Architectural decisions\n\n- Core translations are explicit through `CoreTranslator`; module translations\n  are restricted to the owning module through `ModuleTranslator`.\n- Locale selection revision and catalog snapshot revision are separate. Locale\n  selection changes do not mutate catalog contents.\n- Catalog replacement is copy-on-write behind `Arc`; preparation is all-or-\n  nothing for source read/JSON parse failures, while per-entry diagnostics keep\n  valid siblings.\n- Graph/profile/reload commits carry a prepared `LocaleEngine` candidate and\n  commit graph, selection, profile settings, and catalog state together.\n- Shell-created components receive the committed immutable snapshot. Direct\n  component fixtures may still load their own graph catalog paths.\n- Theme state is a complete shell-owned rendered snapshot; provider mirrors\n  cannot overwrite rendered theme facts.\n\n## Tests and results\n\nPassed:\n\n- `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` — 11 tests.\n- `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-theme` — 23 tests.\n- `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-config --lib` — 41 tests.\n- `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check`.\n- `git diff --check`.\n\nNot passed: shell/workspace compilation is blocked before `mesh-core-shell` by\nthe pre-existing `InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`. The broader handoff also\nrecords pre-existing `ThemeModeMetadata`, extension-module inference, and\nscripting ownership/move errors. Do not claim shell tests passed.\n\n## Unresolved problems\n\n- Implement the next language-pack/default/provenance/precedence backlog item.\n- Remaining localization items include durable revision-checked locale profile\n  transactions, ABI/capability alignment, centralized metadata/miss handling,\n  bounded off-shell compilation, CLI/LSP/extraction/doctor support, and locale\n  policies.\n- Full shell validation remains blocked by the pre-existing service/module/\n  scripting compile failures.\n\n## Exact next steps\n\n1. Re-read `docs/BACKLOG.md`, `.planning/STATUS.md`, the latest\n   `.planning/log/2026-08.md` entry, and the relevant localization spec/audit.\n2. Inspect current manifest i18n contributions, installed-graph contribution\n   indexing, and `LocaleEngine` lookup APIs.\n3. Implement a bounded increment for ordered language-pack chains, per-module\n   defaults, source provenance, and duplicate precedence validation. Keep\n   behavior module-scoped and preserve last-known-good snapshots.\n4. Add focused foundation/graph tests, run them through the Nix dev shell, run\n   formatting and `git diff --check`, and retry shell checks only after\n   accounting for the known blockers.\n5. If the increment is complete, remove its item from `docs/BACKLOG.md`, append\n   the result to `.planning/log/2026-08.md`, update `.planning/STATUS.md`, and\n   refresh this handoff without touching `codex-loop.log`.\n# Architecture\n\n## System overview\n\nMESH is a modular Wayland shell runtime. A root module graph selects frontend\nand backend modules; frontend `.mesh` files compile into component trees;\nsandboxed Luau code implements component and service behavior; typed interfaces\nconnect consumers to providers; and the shell maps retained UI state to Wayland\nsurfaces. The architecture follows a microkernel-like policy boundary without\nturning MESH into a process or privilege supervisor.\n\n## Current runtime flow\n\n```text\nconfig/module.json + optional active profile\n        │\n        ▼\nmodule discovery and installed graph\n        │\n        ├──► interface registry ──► selected backend providers\n        │                                  │\n        ▼                                  ▼\nfrontend entrypoints ──► .mesh compiler ──► Luau service proxies\n        │\n        ▼\ncomponent/runtime tree\n        │\n        ├──► style and Taffy layout\n        ├──► retained render data and software paint\n        ├──► input, focus, gestures, and accessibility\n        └──► diagnostics and profiling\n        │\n        ▼\npresentation layer ──► Wayland surfaces\n```\n\nThe CLI creates `mesh_core_shell::Shell`. The shell discovers modules, loads\nthe installed graph, registers interface contracts and providers, compiles\nfrontend roots, executes service/component Luau, renders surfaces, and handles\nWayland events.\n\nDuring the profile migration, absence of `config/active-profile` preserves the\nlegacy root-graph decisions. When it exists, `config/profiles/<id>.json` owns\nthe desired composition: explicit roots/background services/providers/resources\nseed an activation closure, and declared dependencies plus sole providers are\ninferred before contributions are indexed. Installed source remains available\neven when it is not part of that closure.\n\n## Platform boundary\n\nThe core owns mechanisms that must remain consistent and enforceable:\n\n- module loading, validation, graph resolution, and lifecycle;\n- component and service execution;\n- typed state, method, and event transport;\n- capabilities, sandbox policy, and failure isolation;\n- layout, rendering, input, accessibility, and Wayland presentation;\n- generic persistence primitives and structured diagnostics.\n\nModules own policy and finished experiences. Settings UI, developer tools,\npackage tooling, panels, launchers, themes, and system integrations should be\nreplaceable modules with no hidden privilege.\n\n## Key abstractions\n\n| Abstraction | Ownership | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ModuleManifest` | `mesh-core-module` | Canonical `module.json` representation |\n| `SystemResourceCatalog` | `mesh-core-resources` | Cached host XDG icon themes and font families |\n| `InstalledModuleGraph` | `mesh-core-module` | Resolved modules, interfaces, providers, and diagnostics |\n| `InterfaceContract` | `mesh-core-service` | Typed service state, methods, events, and shared types |\n| `ScriptContext` | `mesh-core-scripting` | Isolated frontend Luau execution context |\n| `BackendRuntime` | `mesh-core-scripting` | Luau service-provider execution and host APIs |\n| `WidgetNode` | `mesh-core-elements` | Retained UI node with style, layout, and semantics |\n| `FrontendSurfaceComponent` | `mesh-core-shell` | Component instance attached to a shell surface |\n| `Shell` | `mesh-core-shell` | Development-runtime integration point |\n\n## Service architecture\n\nA component depends on an interface name and compatible version rather than a\nprovider module ID. Providers own their domain state and implement declared\nmethods and events. The core validates and transports those records; it should\nnot compute audio-, network-, power-, or settings-specific policy.\n\nProvider selection is explicit when several compatible providers are enabled.\nWhen exactly one compatible provider exists, the current graph may select it\nautomatically. Missing optional services degrade locally; missing required\nservices are graph diagnostics.\n\n## Shell profiles\n\nNamed shell profiles replace a single root layout decision. A profile selects\nroot component instances, surface placement, ambiguous service providers,\nresources, root background services,\nand profile-scoped configuration. Required services remain inferred from\ncomponent contracts.\n\nLive switching must be transactional: validate and prepare a candidate graph,\npreserve identical service instances, initialize new surfaces, commit the\nvisible switch, and only then remove orphaned runtime objects. Durable\nservice-owned data remains shared while configuration is profile-scoped.\n\nThe root `config/module.json` remains the installed-module inventory and legacy\nfallback. Profiles are the active composition boundary once `active-profile`\nexists. Typed profile/package services for replaceable settings frontends are\nstill target architecture.\n\n## Directory structure\n\n```text\ncrates/\n  core/\n    foundation/     cross-cutting contracts, data, and host resource discovery\n    extension/      modules and service interfaces\n    ui/             components, elements, interaction, animation\n    frontend/       compilation, hosting, rendering\n    runtime/        sandboxed Luau and backend execution\n    platform/       Wayland integration\n    shell/          running-shell composition\n  tools/            CLI and LSP\nmodules/             shipped editable module sources\nconfig/              current development graph and settings\ndocs/spec/           public shipped/target contract\ndocs/                current author and maintainer guidance\ndocs/BACKLOG.md      the single list of open work\n.planning/STATUS.md  what is in flight now\n.planning/log/       dated, append-only history and measurements\n.planning/archive/   frozen superseded records\n```\n\nDetailed crate dependency rules are documented in\n[crate boundaries](../crate-boundaries.md).\n# MESH Specification\n\nThis directory is the **single unified specification** for the MESH module\nplatform. It supersedes the older scattered design docs (`module-system.md`,\n`extensibility.md`, `installation.md`, `icon-system.md`, `font-system.md`,\n`theming/`, `settings/`, `health.md`, `component-configuration.md`,\n`module-vocabulary.md`, and the root `spec/pluggable-backend.md`), which have\nbeen deleted. If an older document or commit message contradicts this spec,\nthis spec wins.\n\nEach part is marked with an implementation status per section:\n\n- **Shipped** — implemented and tested in the current tree.\n- **Target** — decided design, not yet (fully) implemented.\n\nThe confirmed direction records (2026-07-01 and 2026-07-16) encode one sparse\nsettings model, user icon-pack modules and pack chains, props everywhere,\nclosed core plus open contribution namespaces, path and Git installation,\nload-time theme cascade, minimal font packs, automation IPC, and thin MCP.\n\nThe 2026-07-16 platform direction further establishes MESH as a shell-building\nplatform: directly editable modules provide components and services; one module\nexports one primary public unit; named profiles compose root components and\nservice choices; configuration is profile-scoped while durable service data is\nshared; and settings, developer tools, and package experiences are replaceable\nmodules rather than privileged core UI.\n\n## Parts\n\n| Part | Covers |\n| ---- | ------ |\n| [01 — Module System](01-module-system.md) | Vocabulary, `module.json`, kinds, contracts, providers, profiles, capabilities, lifecycle, trust |\n| [02 — Installation & Health](02-installation.md) | Installer v1 (path + git), directories, doctor, health states, diagnostics |\n| [03 — Components & Props](03-components.md) | `.mesh` component model, the `<props>` block, projections, precedence |\n| [04 — Styling & Theming](04-styling.md) | Theme packs, tokens, load-time cascade, module theme contributions, modes |\n| [05 — Icons](05-icons.md) | Semantic names, icon-pack modules, vocabulary index, resolution chain, variable axes |\n| [06 — Fonts](06-fonts.md) | Font-pack modules, logical roles, `--font-*` tokens, resolution |\n| [07 — Localization](07-i18n.md) | Module catalogs, language packs, lookup chain, plurals, RTL |\n| [08 — Settings](08-settings.md) | The single sparse settings store, namespaces, precedence, generated UI |\n| [09 — Accessibility](09-accessibility.md) | Semantic tree, roles, names, states, AccessKit, diagnostics |\n| [10 — Keyboard](10-keyboard.md) | Focus traversal, activation keys, keybind contributions, `keyboard_mode` |\n| [11 — Automation IPC](11-automation-ipc.md) | Capability-gated IPC: semantic tree, element actions, surfaces, settings |\n| [12 — MCP for LLMs](12-mcp.md) | The thin `mesh-mcp` binary over the automation IPC |\n\n## How the parts compose\n\n```\n                 shell profile (01, target)\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                       module.json  (01)\n        identity · kind · uses · provides · implements\n                              │\n        ┌──────────┬──────────┼───────────┬─────────────┐\n        ▼          ▼          ▼           ▼             ▼\n   interfaces   resource    .mesh      keybinds     capabilities\n   + providers   packs    components     (10)          (01)\n      (01)     (05,06,07)  + props (03)\n        │          │          │\n        │          │          ▼\n        │          │     <props> ──► prop() CSS · props.* Lua · settings rows (08)\n        │          │          │\n        ▼          ▼          ▼\n   settings store (08) ◄── user decisions: theme/mode, pack chains, locale,\n        │                   keybind overrides, per-module + per-instance props\n        ▼\n   theme cascade (04) ──► style resolution ──► semantic tree (09)\n                                                    │\n                                     keyboard focus (10) ─ AccessKit (09)\n                                                    │\n                                        automation IPC (11) ──► mesh-mcp (12)\n```\n\nOne mental model repeats everywhere:\n\n1. **Semantic names, not concrete assets.** Templates use logical icon names,\n   font roles, theme tokens, and i18n keys; packs map them to real assets.\n2. **Ordered chains with fallback** for multi-active resources (icons, fonts,\n   language packs); **winner-takes-all** for coherence-critical ones (theme).\n3. **Declared defaults, sparse user overrides.** Modules and packs declare\n   defaults; the settings store holds only what the user changed.\n4. **More specific wins.** Author default → user global → author instance →\n   user per-instance, everywhere a value can be layered.\n5. **The core wires, modules work.** Rust routes generic records; behavior\n   lives in Luau modules and declarative JSON contracts.\n6. **Profiles compose; they do not supervise.** A profile selects roots,\n   providers, resources, and scoped configuration without becoming a process\n   manager or privilege system.\n\n## Related reference docs (not part of this spec)\n\n- [`../architecture/overview.md`](../architecture/overview.md) — codebase and\n  runtime orientation.\n- [`../frontend/mesh-syntax.md`](../frontend/mesh-syntax.md) — `.mesh` syntax reference.\n- [`../frontend/elements.md`](../frontend/elements.md) — native element taxonomy.\n- [`../modules/README.md`](../modules/README.md) — shipped module index.\n# MESH — Active Backlog\n\nThe single list of what is open. Specifications describe contracts\n([`spec/`](spec/)); guides describe current behavior; history and measurements\nlive in [`.planning/log/`](../.planning/log/).\n\n**Items here say what to do and why it is not done — nothing else.** Progress\nnarratives, benchmark numbers, and completed items belong in the log. When an\nitem lands, delete it from this file and write its record in\n[`.planning/log/`](../.planning/log/README.md).\n\nVerify an older item against the source before starting it; later work\nsometimes lands without updating a checkbox.\n\n**Detail** for items carried over from before 2026-07-28 is in\n[`.planning/log/backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md`](../.planning/log/backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md)\n— a verbatim snapshot of this file with the full progress history. Items marked\n_(detail: …)_ name the section to search for there. Section letters (A–V) refer\nto [`.planning/log/sections.md`](../.planning/log/sections.md); `→ vX.Y` markers\nare from the retired milestone scheme and are kept only as rough sequencing.\n\n---\n\n## Shell features\n\n## Section audit feature checklist\n\nThese are the feature-level items extracted from the section improvement audits.\nThe audit files retain the process maps, evidence, design detail, and regression\nmatrices; this checklist is the canonical place to track implementation.\n\n### 1. Core foundation contracts\n\nThe six primary findings in this audit shipped on 2026-08-20 and are recorded in\nthe monthly log, so they are intentionally absent from the open backlog.\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/01-core-foundation-contracts/improvements.md)\n\n### 2. Module system and installation\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/02-module-system-and-installation/improvements.md)\n\n\n### 3. Service contracts\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/03-service-contracts/improvements.md)\n\n\n### 4. Themes\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/04-themes/improvements.md)\n\n### 5. Localization and i18n\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/05-localization-i18n/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Add targeted ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance, and duplicate precedence checks.\n- [ ] Route locale changes through one durable revision-checked settings/profile transaction.\n- [ ] Align `mesh.i18n`, `mesh.locale`, runtime/LSP ABI, and read/write capability enforcement.\n- [ ] Centralize localized metadata and visible miss handling with owner, fallback, source, and snapshot revision.\n- [ ] Open bounded catalogs through contained no-follow source handles and compile them off the shell thread.\n- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/extraction/doctor tooling from the canonical graph, and implement or explicitly reject `<i18n>` blocks.\n- [ ] Add manual, follow-system, and per-profile locale policies through the same prepare/persist/commit path.\n\n### 6. Host resources and icon packs\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/06-host-resources-and-icon-packs/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Build icon and font resources only from the prepared graph/profile and atomically retain the last-known-good resource snapshot.\n- [ ] Preserve declared pack order and reject duplicate canonical IDs, aliases, and ambiguous ownership deterministically.\n- [ ] Open module resource assets through contained, no-follow, bounded handles.\n- [ ] Implement canonical semantic and dash-generalized icon fallback resolution with required/optional diagnostics.\n- [ ] Preserve typed multicolor mappings, source color policy, and mapping provenance.\n- [ ] Validate complete packs off the render thread and publish them atomically without partial registry state.\n- [ ] Propagate resource revisions/fingerprints through negative lookup, glyph, font, text-layout, and renderer caches.\n- [ ] Implement font-pack runtime resolution with logical roles, pack-qualified roles, faces, coverage, and typography tokens.\n- [ ] Unify XDG icon/font discovery and lookup into one ordered, refreshable host catalog.\n- [ ] Prepare resource parsing and asset handles away from shell/render threads with bounded cancellation.\n- [ ] Generate runtime, CLI, LSP, doctor, and debug resource explanations from one effective snapshot.\n- [ ] Add a resource coverage advisor for semantic vocabulary and font-script gaps without silently reordering user chains.\n\n### 7. Component language\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/07-component-language/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Make local component imports module-relative, canonicalized, contained, and symlink-safe across compiler, watcher, and LSP paths.\n- [ ] Resolve recursive imports by owner scope and canonical target, with collision and cycle diagnostics.\n- [ ] Replace lossy block extraction with a span-preserving top-level parser that validates required, unique, attributed blocks and `<i18n>` policy.\n- [ ] Parse interpolations and control-flow braces with a real lexer/parser that rejects malformed expressions and preserves spans.\n- [ ] Add semantic validation linking `<props>`, `prop()` references, child props, visibility, types, and CSS domains.\n- [ ] Select the highest valid value across prop/configuration layers while retaining invalid overrides only in diagnostics.\n- [ ] Make JSON-to-prop conversion scalar-only until an explicit structured prop type exists.\n- [ ] Normalize and validate `PropDef` constraints, units, options, tokens, and CSS-domain values once.\n- [ ] Replace line-oriented script/import/symbol scans with one Luau lexer/parser and source metadata.\n- [ ] Carry reliable source spans through component AST nodes, compiler errors, CLI diagnostics, and LSP output.\n\n### 8. UI element core\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/08-ui-element-core/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Normalize roles, names, descriptions, hidden state, ARIA aliases, focus, relationships, and visibility after child construction and publish the semantic snapshot.\n- [ ] Use visible descendant text in accessible-name precedence and preserve hidden-child and locale behavior.\n- [ ] Generate one typed pseudo-state table for indexing, mutation, matching, invalidation, diagnostics, and tests.\n- [ ] Make compiler inheritance matching consume the actual element state.\n- [ ] Make retained layout transactional, preserve last-known-good geometry, and retry after failure.\n- [ ] Use one stateful input dispatcher with pointer capture, press-origin identity, activation semantics, focus eligibility, and invalidation output.\n- [ ] Generate element types, source/runtime tags, contracts, events, attributes, style hooks, and accessibility defaults from one schema.\n- [ ] Detect CSS custom-property cycles with structured diagnostics and specified invalid-value fallback.\n- [ ] Preserve explicit/inherited property masks through retained style resolution and targeted restyle.\n- [ ] Derive accessibility focus from canonical live focus state during snapshot generation.\n- [ ] Include all shaping inputs and resource/measurer revisions in text measurement contexts and cache keys.\n- [ ] Make popover placement tokens and trigger/surface relationships typed, validated, and observable across promotion.\n- [ ] Introduce an immutable frame snapshot with phase stamps, semantic diffs, stable identities, and property-based invariant tests.\n\n### 9. Interaction and motion\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/09-interaction-and-motion/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Share visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and target filtering across interaction, rendering, focus, scrolling, tooltips, and accessibility.\n- [ ] Use one affine transform/clip contract for hit testing, paint bounds, scrolling, and focus geometry.\n- [ ] Prevent disabled and inert nodes, including descendants and captured targets, from receiving activation.\n- [ ] Preserve keyframe progress across pause/resume and iteration-boundary changes.\n- [ ] Add a `MotionPolicy` snapshot for reduced motion across transitions, keyframes, scrolling, inertia, tooltips, and surfaces.\n- [ ] Consolidate focus, pointer capture, press origin, gesture ownership, and scroll ownership into one transaction.\n- [ ] Preserve the previous discrete value during visibility transitions until the transition completes.\n- [ ] Propagate validated per-keyframe easing through component parsing, shell state, and animation sampling.\n- [ ] Keep interaction policy in a renderer-neutral frame/state-machine contract with typed decisions and dirty outputs.\n- [ ] Implement `box-shadow` parsing with structured errors or reject it before the public animation API.\n- [ ] Give animation instances stable identity and explicit replacement, cancellation, and reversal semantics.\n- [ ] Introduce an `InteractionFrame` shared by input, state, style invalidation, layout, animation, paint, and semantics.\n\n### 10. Frontend compiler and host\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/10-frontend-compiler-and-host/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Reject absolute, traversal, and symlinked frontend entrypoint/import paths outside the module root.\n- [ ] Keep service payloads Rust-owned unless the consumer has the resolved read capability; event subscriptions alone must not expose state.\n- [ ] Reload primary and contribution roots together as one atomic catalog generation.\n- [ ] Validate contribution roots against interface requirements, availability, and version ranges.\n- [ ] Validate root and nested expression scopes uniformly using parser/runtime symbol tables and source spans.\n- [ ] Compile expressions once into shared semantics so preview, live, translation, and composition paths behave identically.\n- [ ] Split the frontend host into a renderer-neutral ABI and shell adapters with typed capability-scoped effects.\n- [ ] Build one reverse dependency graph over primary and contribution roots for invalidation.\n- [ ] Scope local component aliases by owner and canonical source, rejecting collisions.\n- [ ] Replace line-oriented Luau symbol discovery with parser/compiler metadata or remove it as a source of truth.\n- [ ] Make expression scanning UTF-8 safe and diagnose non-ASCII input instead of panicking.\n- [ ] Publish runtime props transactionally so Rust and Luau cannot diverge after a failed update.\n- [ ] Preserve typed diagnostic categories and source spans through AST, compiler, shell, LSP, and debug paths.\n- [ ] Prevent stale catalog rollback from overwriting a newer generation using a coordinator or compare-and-swap.\n- [ ] Enforce distinct popover and overflow child-surface lifecycle, focus, dismissal, placement, and ownership semantics.\n- [ ] Dispatch frontend `mount` and `unmount` hooks in every initialization, reload, deactivation, and replacement path.\n- [ ] Contain expression/runtime failures with last-known-good trees or bounded error placeholders and actionable diagnostics.\n- [ ] Publish normalized public-prop schemas and validate imported/contribution props at the import boundary.\n- [ ] Reject local/module import alias collisions or resolve them through one typed import namespace.\n- [ ] Introduce a coherent revisioned `FrontendFrame` boundary for tree, catalog, runtime, services, invalidation, diagnostics, and effects.\n- [ ] Emit immutable compiled frontend revisions and authorize typed effects only when catalog/runtime revisions still match.\n\n### 11. Luau runtime and sandbox\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/11-luau-runtime-and-sandbox/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Enforce one sandbox/resource policy for every Luau realm, including instruction, memory, output, queue, storage, and child-process budgets.\n- [ ] Keep service state Rust-owned, generation-aware, and capability-filtered instead of exposing shared globals.\n- [ ] Route every backend early return, callback failure, stop, and crash through idempotent cleanup and one terminal lifecycle record.\n- [ ] Make unsubscribe-safe event iteration and independent subscriber failure reporting explicit.\n- [ ] Give stream subprocesses stable identities, bounded queues, exit events, awaited reaping, and shutdown semantics.\n- [ ] Move `mesh.exec` off the async backend loop and enforce cancellation, deadlines, executable policy, and output limits.\n- [ ] Replace basename executable grants with canonical path/argument capability policy.\n- [ ] Move default runtime storage to secure durable XDG state with permissions, quotas, atomic recovery, and revisioned writers.\n- [ ] Make backend command dispatch typed, correlated, transactional, coalescable, generation-aware, and bounded.\n- [ ] Gate event and side-effect queues through one typed authorization/resource boundary before acceptance.\n- [ ] Represent locale as a host-owned per-context cell updated with the translation snapshot.\n- [ ] Treat Luau `nil` writes as explicit deletions in Rust state and dependency invalidation.\n- [ ] Restrict provider event publication to provider-owned handles and isolate subscriber callback failures.\n- [ ] Preserve one stable backend `self` and event/storage handles for each runtime generation.\n- [ ] Publish validated initial provider state before atomically marking the provider ready.\n- [ ] Compile and swap a fresh Luau environment on reload, preserving only explicit durable storage.\n- [ ] Stage backend top-level execution so host side effects cannot escape a failed `start(self)`.\n- [ ] Bound command/event ingress, JSON depth/bytes, event counts, and aggregate runtime resource budgets.\n- [ ] Separate effective read and control grants and enforce them through both proxy and shell paths.\n- [ ] Reconcile changed poll intervals after every stream callback.\n- [ ] Return recoverable host-installation errors instead of panicking during backend setup.\n- [ ] Introduce a per-module `RuntimeSession` combining realm, host, resource broker, lifecycle, state, supervisor, health, backoff, and quarantine.\n\n### 12. Rendering and paint\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/12-rendering-and-paint/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Make `PixelCanvasSession` storage-safe so mapped drawing surfaces cannot outlive backing allocations.\n- [ ] Lower asymmetric four-edge borders and four-corner radii correctly.\n- [ ] Use one cumulative affine transform/clip model across paint, damage, blur, descendants, and interaction.\n- [ ] Lower node opacity and blend mode as isolated compositing groups instead of per primitive.\n- [ ] Include paint-order/topology changes in display-list generations and stable equal-z ordering.\n- [ ] Unify dirty contracts and paint signatures for all content/style fields, including controls, text, icons, and variables.\n- [ ] Include font/resource revisions in text style painting and all glyph/font/text cache keys.\n- [ ] Make backdrop blur an explicit renderer/backend capability with validated fallback behavior.\n- [ ] Use one fractional-scale rounding policy across layout, buffer, paint, copy, and protocol damage.\n- [ ] Preserve release batch/barrier metrics in diagnostics and debug telemetry.\n- [ ] Validate caller lineage before trusting generation shortcuts.\n- [ ] Include resource revisions in retained-present decisions and cache invalidation.\n- [ ] Preserve pending damage and avoid acknowledging unshown frames after presentation errors.\n- [ ] Move resource decode and rasterization off the frame thread through a bounded resource broker.\n- [ ] Enforce byte- and dimension-based cache budgets for decoded assets, fonts, glyphs, text, Skia, PixelBuffer, and SHM allocations.\n- [ ] Introduce a typed frame paint plan with immutable inputs, topology, transforms, effect regions, replay spans, and exact logical/device damage.\n\n### 13. Surface policy and configuration\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/13-surface-policy-and-configuration/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Enforce the author-only `promotable` guard for settings, IPC, and live role changes.\n- [ ] Remove or formally protect `promotable` from user-overridable/ejected settings.\n- [ ] Validate manifest surface enums and role constraints with canonical parsers before graph resolution.\n- [ ] Include blur and window decorations in semantic presentation change detection.\n- [ ] Route settings-driven role reloads through the same transactional transition supervisor as explicit promotion.\n- [ ] Share role-field metadata across manifest diagnostics, settings validation, ejection, and protocol lowering.\n- [ ] Preserve localization identity and distinguish effective values from overrides during configuration ejection.\n- [ ] Use typed unmeasured/content/surface/wire extents at the shell/presentation boundary.\n- [ ] Replace split surface field lists with a revisioned semantic policy diff and generation.\n- [ ] Build the policy compiler producing declared contracts, effective snapshots, and typed transition plans.\n\n### 14. Wayland platform and presentation\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/14-wayland-platform-and-presentation/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Return typed create/configure/present/lost outcomes, cache only accepted generations, and retain damage for missing surfaces.\n- [ ] Route close, dismiss, parent destruction, role replacement, and connection loss through one idempotent teardown supervisor.\n- [ ] Replace incomplete presentation hashes and warm region caches with typed semantic diffs and object/configure/frame generations.\n- [ ] Preserve click-grab seat and serial identity through input normalization, popup requests, and one-shot configuration.\n- [ ] Gate popup reposition by negotiated protocol version and validate popup role, parent, identity, and reparenting.\n- [ ] Use one authoritative logical extent for paint, buffer validation, viewport, regions, attach, and queries.\n- [ ] Commit opaque, blur, and other surface-state changes even when pixel damage is empty.\n- [ ] Separate frame-callback waiting from buffer-release backpressure and prevent hot retry loops.\n- [ ] Make input ownership per seat and cancel pointer, touch, gesture, focus, and repeat transactions during teardown.\n- [ ] Validate buffer length/stride/scale, propagate attach/region errors, expose connection loss, and reap clipboard children.\n- [ ] Replace the recorder backend with a deterministic protocol-state lifecycle simulator plus a focused live compositor matrix.\n- [ ] Add full pointer-button identity and IME/text-input-v3 support while preserving normalized Escape behavior.\n- [ ] Build the transactional, capability-aware presentation engine with version reporting, reactive popups, multi-output membership, per-seat input, and shared SHM/dmabuf/GPU frame contracts.\n\n### 15. Shell core and orchestration\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/15-shell-core-and-orchestration/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one immutable activation plan and atomic runtime-generation commit.\n- [ ] Reconcile backend enable/disable and every graph delta through the same activation coordinator.\n- [ ] Tag backend tasks, bridges, messages, events, results, and restart deadlines with activation generations and provider epochs.\n- [ ] Make frontend/backend stop, unmount, storage flush, worker join, and shutdown cleanup explicit and idempotent.\n- [ ] Give detached workers owned wake handles and lifecycle guards that outlive the eventfd until join.\n- [ ] Isolate component/runtime callback, tick, build, render, and reload failures with placeholders, diagnostics, and quarantine.\n- [ ] Replace static startup watching with a managed generation-aware watch set and bounded polling fallback.\n- [ ] Process CoreRequest effects through one fair bounded scheduler with causal budgets and cycle detection.\n- [ ] Publish provider unavailable/recovery transitions from committed provider generations.\n- [ ] Route control-plane writes through declared durable revisions and ordered settings/theme/locale effect batches.\n- [ ] Distinguish intentional legacy/no-profile operation from invalid configured graph/profile recovery.\n- [ ] Connect package journal commit/rollback to runtime activation so disk and live state share one recoverable transaction.\n- [ ] Introduce `ActiveSnapshot` with candidate preview in hidden surfaces and an explicit quiescing-to-stopped shutdown state machine.\n\n### 16. Developer and authoring tools\n\n[Audit](../.planning/log/sections/16-developer-and-authoring-tools/improvements.md)\n\n- [ ] Make uninstall path-safe and reject traversal/symlink escapes before recursive deletion.\n# Status\n\n**Updated:** 2026-08-22\n\nThis page describes the present. History lives in [`log/`](log/); open work\nlives in [`docs/BACKLOG.md`](../docs/BACKLOG.md).\n\n## Now\n\nTheme availability now comes from the enabled installed graph: canonical theme\ncontributions produce owner-scoped descriptors with validated modes/defaults and\nmodule-root-relative source handles, and runtime registration no longer scans\n`config/themes`. Theme selection and reload now use graph identities and\nsymlink-safe, bounded UTF-8 source opening. Transactional theme/profile commits\nnow prepare candidates before profile commit and retain the last-known-good\nsnapshot after reload failures. The active snapshot now composes recovery\ndefaults, the selected graph pack/mode, module-owned frontend layers, and sparse\nuser token overrides with per-value provenance. Theme selection is durable and\npublishes its selected mode, source fingerprint, and snapshot revision; reload\npolling follows content fingerprints even after a source directory changes.\nComponent and theme CSS now lower through one restricted selector AST; theme\nstate/class rules execute through the same element matcher, and malformed\ntheme CSS is rejected with bounded declaration parsing. Theme and component\nkeyframes now share runtime lowering, token aliases resolve transitively with\ncycle diagnostics, and computed style snapshots carry inherited custom\nproperties. The rendered theme snapshot is authoritative for the shell's\nmode, color scheme, contrast, effective tokens, provenance, and revisioned\n`ThemeChanged`/`TokenChanged` interface events; provider mirrors cannot replace\nit. ThemeEngine now retains immutable active snapshot identity and rejects\nambiguous registrations. Explicit manual, system-following, and scheduled mode\npolicies now use the same transaction path; policy changes are persisted in the\ntheme snapshot and trigger the same revisioned event/invalidation flow. Locale\ncatalogs now expose explicit\nmodule/core translators: templates, Luau, manifests, debug metadata, and\ngenerated component settings use the owning module without global key leakage.\nLocale selection is now normalized and immutable at the snapshot boundary:\nBCP 47 tags derive parent and terminal fallback chains, direction and revision\nare published with the locale service state, and invalid runtime tags are\nrejected. Typed catalog compilation now preserves valid siblings, validates\nplaceholder parity, and resolves bounded plural/select entries in Rust and\nLuau. LocaleEngine now publishes revisioned immutable catalog Arcs with an\nexplicit replacement seam. Graph discovery, cached graph loading,\nmodule enable/disable, provider selection, profile switching, and settings\nlocale reloads prepare complete catalog candidates before committing selection,\ngraph, profile, and runtime state; failed reads/parses retain the\nlast-known-good graph and catalog. Shell-created components consume the\ncommitted Arc instead of rereading graph files, while standalone component\nfixtures retain their source-backed fallback. The next localization item is\nordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance,\nand duplicate precedence checks.\n\nThe first four Section 2 items are complete. Package commits publish\nvalidated module trees into a read-only SHA-256 object store and activate\ngeneration-stamped snapshots. Installed-graph and shell discovery resolve the\nactive object set, while editable module trees remain the authoring/update\nsource. Shell runtime instances and catalogs now come only from the validated\nresolved graph, and invalid startup/reload candidates fail closed. Candidate\nupdates explain normalized graph changes before commit, while frontend primary\nentries and extension-point contributions are indexed incrementally with\nmodule-scoped source diagnostics and retryable watch paths. Focused package and\ncatalog tests are added; Cargo\nexecution is currently blocked\nbecause the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is unavailable.\n\nTrust-tier plumbing is now complete for the current installer boundary: lock\nentries carry local/community/core/verified classification and detached\nsignature metadata, `module.sig` signs a canonical module identity/version/\ndigest payload, and root-graph `trustPolicy.keys` verify Ed25519 signatures\nbefore install, staged update review, lock verification, or graph activation.\nInvalid signatures and unknown keys fail closed with scoped diagnostics;\nregistry key distribution remains a future integration.\n\nService payloads now live in a generation-stamped Rust store owned by each\nfrontend script context. Shared Lua VMs no longer receive `__mesh_svc_*`\nglobals or marker tables; proxy reads are capability-filtered and copy values\nout of the store, service state is read-only, and the shell skips payload\ndelivery to contexts without the matching read grant. The focused scripting\ntests are present, but Cargo execution remains blocked by the unavailable\n`sha2` registry cache and network access.\n\nCorrelated service calls now complete end to end. Luau interface methods return\ncooperative tickets with `poll`/`await` snapshots and caller-driven\ncancellation; call IDs and originating instance identities survive shell\ndispatch, throttling, backend coalescing, provider replacement, and terminal\nresult delivery. Missing providers, stale generations, timeouts, failures,\nsupersession, and cancellation all settle the originating ticket exactly once.\n\nOptimistic contract-bound service writes are now CallId- and provider-generation\nscoped transactions. Successful calls retain their overlay until a matching\nprovider snapshot confirms the value; failed, timed-out, cancelled, superseded,\nunavailable, and replaced-provider calls roll back to the prior visible state.\nOlder completions cannot roll back a newer write, and chained failures unwind to\nthe last provider value. The focused shell regression is present, but Cargo\nexecution remains blocked by the unavailable `sha2` registry cache and network\naccess.\n\nService contracts now compile explicit `read`, per-event subscription, and\nper-method capability policies. The resolved contract policy is enforced at\nfrontend activation diagnostics, Luau proxy creation and operation calls,\nevent subscription, cached/live state delivery, and shell dispatch, including\nthird-party interface names; legacy contracts retain a narrow compatibility\nfallback. Policy changes are included in contract compatibility diffs.\n\nService registration now serializes contract/provider mutations through one\nlock-protected snapshot and publishes generation-stamped `ResolvedServiceCatalog`\nviews. Registry resolution, runtime catalog creation, frontend binding,\nprofile startup, and debug inspection consume one coherent contract/provider\nepoch; an already-published catalog remains unchanged when later registrations\narrive.\n\nThe transitional untyped `ServiceRegistry` has been removed. Shell service\nauthority now lives only in the generation-stamped interface catalog; no\n`TypeId`/`Any` backend store remains.\n\nInterface registrations now compile one immutable `CompiledContract` artifact\nwith canonical identity, parsed recursive schemas, normalized operation policy,\nbehavior metadata, schema/policy/behavior fingerprints, and declaration\nprovenance. Catalog resolutions carry that artifact while retaining the raw\ncontract view for migration callers.\n\nThe compiled artifact now drives deterministic Luau consumer type declarations,\nprovider `start(self)` stubs, in-memory mocks, and standalone Markdown contract\ndocumentation. Generators return content without filesystem access so CLI/LSP\ncallers can choose their own output policy.\n\nContracts now declare `capabilities.featureGroups` with explicit optional or\nrequired semantics. Provider feature advertisements are normalized per provider\nin the generation-stamped catalog: optional groups degrade without rejecting the\nprovider, while missing required groups make the provider unavailable and are\nreported in interface lookup diagnostics.\n\nBidirectional contract diffs now expose a typed `CompatibilityClassification`\nwith independent consumer and provider classes. A change can therefore be\nadditive for existing consumers while breaking old providers without collapsing\nthose outcomes into one label.\n\nService calls now share one `CallId` transaction across Luau awaitable tickets,\nshell routing, backend deadlines/cancellation, coalescing supersession,\ntracing/debug records, and optimistic state rollback. Each terminal outcome is\nsettled once and older provider generations cannot affect newer transactions.\n\nService contracts now validate recursive named fields, arrays, optional values,\nand `Result` returns across state, events, method inputs, and provider outputs.\nInvalid snapshots preserve the last-known-good state, invalid events and\ncommands are rejected at their boundaries, and provider result violations settle\nas contract failures. Luau method proxies enforce arity and argument types with\nproxy-specific colon-call detection. Cargo verification remains blocked by the\nunavailable `sha2` registry cache and network access.\n\nProvider runtimes now prepare privately and activate only after lifecycle\nreadiness plus a valid initial snapshot for stateful interfaces. Startup,\nsupervised restart, live provider switch, and profile candidates all buffer\nprepared output and replay it once after commit; invalid preparation leaves the\ncurrent provider and state in place. Health transitions are published after the\nnew runtime is active.\n\nBackend state, interface events, command results, and lifecycle transitions now\nshare active-provider and terminal-generation filtering. Late messages from\nstopped or replaced providers are rejected, while explicit stop/failure paths\npublish unavailable state and health through normal service delivery.\n\nService compatibility now diffs the executable contract in both consumer and\nprovider directions, including recursive named types, identity/version,\nevent-payload additions, `coalesce`, and `stateBinding`. Revision-only update\nplanning resolves external contracts from the candidate Git revision before\nclassification, and provider-side breakage is reported independently. Cargo\nverification remains blocked by the unavailable `sha2` registry cache and\nnetwork access; formatting and whitespace checks pass.\n\nContract identity is now canonicalized when compiled and at every graph,\nruntime, diagnostics, update, and LSP index boundary. Short aliases resolve to\nthe same `InterfaceContract` artifact as canonical names, including provider\nselection and frontend requirements; empty identities are rejected.\n\nThe guarded `scripts/codex-backlog-runner.sh` can process one unchecked backlog\nitem at a time directly on `main`, using Codex's explicit unrestricted mode for\nunattended turns by default (`CODEX_ALLOW_ALL=0` opts back into workspace-write),\nrequiring one feature commit and rolling to a fresh Codex session at the\nruntime-reported context threshold. It is intentionally blocked by any\npre-existing dirty working tree unless `--allow-dirty` is explicitly supplied\nto recover an interrupted turn.\n\nOn NetworkManager hosts, live Codex network-wait errors trigger a throttled\nattempt to restore the most recently used saved Wi-Fi profile; set\n`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0` to disable it.\n\nThe 16 section improvement audits now have a feature-level checkbox checklist\nin `docs/BACKLOG.md`; implementation detail, evidence, and regression matrices\nremain in each section's `improvements.md`.\n\nPackage install, update, uninstall, and rollback now share the core\n`PackageTransaction` engine: one OS lock, a durable journal, staged candidates,\nfilesystem snapshots, and crash recovery. The completed backlog item and its\nvalidation record are in the 2026-08 log.\n\nUpdate planning now materializes every candidate and unchanged graph member in\nthe transaction workspace, resolves external interface contracts and profile\nclosure from that staged graph, and records approval-filtered capability\ndecisions before any live checkout or replacement.\n\nPer-widget promotion is complete: an embedded retained widget can move between\nits parent surface and an independent `xdg_toplevel` while retaining the shared\ncomponent VM, state, paint, and input path. The backlog item and design record\nare closed; validation details are in the 2026-08 log.\n\nSection 01 improvement 1 is complete: module capability declarations now resolve\nagainst a closed catalog and persisted root-graph approvals before activation;\nrequired grants fail without approval, optional grants default to denied, and\nfrontend/backend runtimes receive only the resolved grant set.\n\nSection 01 improvement 2 is complete: shell operations now pass through one\ntyped registry that validates payloads, required capabilities, and caller\nconstraints. Raw shell events, locale writes, and popover helpers share that\nauthorization path; malformed, unauthorized, unknown, and dropped requests\nproduce structured diagnostics.\n\nSection 01 improvement 3 is complete: shared settings field declarations now\nenforce numeric bounds and canonicalize enum values before deserialization.\nRejected values fall back per field, preserving valid siblings; the runtime\nand LSP settings schema consume the same declarations.\n# Log\n\nDated history. **Append-only** — entries are never edited after the fact.\n\nAn entry that turns out to be wrong is corrected by a *new* entry that says so\nand links back. Past entries hold the baselines that later work is measured\nagainst; rewriting one destroys the comparison that made it worth keeping.\n\n## Files\n\n| File | Covers |\n| --- | --- |\n| `YYYY-MM.md` | The running work log. One file per month, newest entry at the top. |\n| [`performance-log.md`](performance-log.md) | Performance history back to 2026-07-02, and the rejected-experiments table. |\n| [`sections.md`](sections.md) | The current package-oriented concern map. Section letters (A–V) in older records are historical references. |\n| [`backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md`](backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md) | Verbatim snapshot of `docs/BACKLOG.md` before the 2026-07-28 split. Holds the long progress narratives for items that are still open. |\n| `<topic>-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | A single completed effort large enough to deserve its own file, e.g. `interaction-identity-2026-07-28.md`. |\n\nPerformance work goes in `performance-log.md`; everything else goes in the\nmonthly file. When a monthly entry would run past roughly a screen, give it its\nown topic file and leave a one-line pointer in the month.\n\n## Entry format\n\n```markdown\n## 2026-07-28 — short title\n\n`commit-sha` · area: style resolution\n\nWhat changed, in a few sentences. What it means for someone reading this in six\nmonths and deciding whether to touch the same code.\n\n**Measured.** Release, 456-node representative tree, three interleaved runs:\n0.416–0.424ms before versus 0.390–0.397ms after (1.05–1.09x). Gated as\n`shared_theme_defaults_speedup`.\n\n**Left open.** Typed property values and one-time token lowering.\n```\n\nOnly the heading and the first line are required. Add the rest when there is\nsomething real to put in them.\n\n## What earns an entry\n\n- Anything that closes a backlog item — the item leaves the backlog, its record\n  lands here.\n- Any performance change, with its measurement.\n- Any approach that was tried, measured, and abandoned. These are the most\n  valuable entries in the file: they stop the next attempt from re-running a\n  known dead end. Add it to the rejected-experiments table in\n  `performance-log.md` as well when it is performance work.\n- Any decision that changes the shape of the system, with the reasoning.\n\nRoutine commits do not need an entry. `git log` already has them; the log is\nfor the things a commit message is too small to hold.\n\n## Measurements\n\nA performance claim without its workload cannot be reproduced, and an\nunreproducible number is worse than none — it gets trusted anyway. Record:\n\n- build profile (release, unless there is a reason it is not),\n- workload shape and size (node counts, iteration counts, tree structure),\n- before and after as ranges across repeated runs, not single numbers,\n- the gate name, if the speedup is checked in CI.\n\nInterleave before/after runs when the effect is small enough that machine drift\nbetween consecutive batches could account for it, and say that you did.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- backlog headings/items ---'; rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|''^- '\"\\\\[ \\\\]' docs/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- status tail / next locale context ---'; rg -n -C 3 'language-pack|locale|localization|Next' .planning/STATUS.md | sed -n '1,220p'; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- planning rules ---'; sed -n '1,260p' .planning/README.md; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- localization spec ---'; sed -n '1,320p' docs/spec/07-i18n.md; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- latest log locale entries ---'; rg -n -C 4 'language-pack|locale|catalog|translation' .planning/log/2026-08.md | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- backlog headings/items ---'; rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|''^- '\"\\\\[ \\\\]' docs/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '1,240p'; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- status tail / next locale context ---'; rg -n -C 3 'language-pack|locale|localization|Next' .planning/STATUS.md | sed -n '1,220p'; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- planning rules ---'; sed -n '1,260p' .planning/README.md; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- localization spec ---'; sed -n '1,320p' docs/spec/07-i18n.md; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- latest log locale entries ---'; rg -n -C 4 'language-pack|locale|catalog|translation' .planning/log/2026-08.md | sed -n '1,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"--- backlog headings/items ---\n24:## Shell features\n26:## Section audit feature checklist\n32:### 1. Core foundation contracts\n38:### 2. Module system and installation\n43:### 3. Service contracts\n48:### 4. Themes\n52:### 5. Localization and i18n\n56:- [ ] Add targeted ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance, and duplicate precedence checks.\n57:- [ ] Route locale changes through one durable revision-checked settings/profile transaction.\n58:- [ ] Align `mesh.i18n`, `mesh.locale`, runtime/LSP ABI, and read/write capability enforcement.\n59:- [ ] Centralize localized metadata and visible miss handling with owner, fallback, source, and snapshot revision.\n60:- [ ] Open bounded catalogs through contained no-follow source handles and compile them off the shell thread.\n61:- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/extraction/doctor tooling from the canonical graph, and implement or explicitly reject `<i18n>` blocks.\n62:- [ ] Add manual, follow-system, and per-profile locale policies through the same prepare/persist/commit path.\n64:### 6. Host resources and icon packs\n68:- [ ] Build icon and font resources only from the prepared graph/profile and atomically retain the last-known-good resource snapshot.\n69:- [ ] Preserve declared pack order and reject duplicate canonical IDs, aliases, and ambiguous ownership deterministically.\n70:- [ ] Open module resource assets through contained, no-follow, bounded handles.\n71:- [ ] Implement canonical semantic and dash-generalized icon fallback resolution with required/optional diagnostics.\n72:- [ ] Preserve typed multicolor mappings, source color policy, and mapping provenance.\n73:- [ ] Validate complete packs off the render thread and publish them atomically without partial registry state.\n74:- [ ] Propagate resource revisions/fingerprints through negative lookup, glyph, font, text-layout, and renderer caches.\n75:- [ ] Implement font-pack runtime resolution with logical roles, pack-qualified roles, faces, coverage, and typography tokens.\n76:- [ ] Unify XDG icon/font discovery and lookup into one ordered, refreshable host catalog.\n77:- [ ] Prepare resource parsing and asset handles away from shell/render threads with bounded cancellation.\n78:- [ ] Generate runtime, CLI, LSP, doctor, and debug resource explanations from one effective snapshot.\n79:- [ ] Add a resource coverage advisor for semantic vocabulary and font-script gaps without silently reordering user chains.\n81:### 7. Component language\n85:- [ ] Make local component imports module-relative, canonicalized, contained, and symlink-safe across compiler, watcher, and LSP paths.\n86:- [ ] Resolve recursive imports by owner scope and canonical target, with collision and cycle diagnostics.\n87:- [ ] Replace lossy block extraction with a span-preserving top-level parser that validates required, unique, attributed blocks and `<i18n>` policy.\n88:- [ ] Parse interpolations and control-flow braces with a real lexer/parser that rejects malformed expressions and preserves spans.\n89:- [ ] Add semantic validation linking `<props>`, `prop()` references, child props, visibility, types, and CSS domains.\n90:- [ ] Select the highest valid value across prop/configuration layers while retaining invalid overrides only in diagnostics.\n91:- [ ] Make JSON-to-prop conversion scalar-only until an explicit structured prop type exists.\n92:- [ ] Normalize and validate `PropDef` constraints, units, options, tokens, and CSS-domain values once.\n93:- [ ] Replace line-oriented script/import/symbol scans with one Luau lexer/parser and source metadata.\n94:- [ ] Carry reliable source spans through component AST nodes, compiler errors, CLI diagnostics, and LSP output.\n96:### 8. UI element core\n100:- [ ] Normalize roles, names, descriptions, hidden state, ARIA aliases, focus, relationships, and visibility after child construction and publish the semantic snapshot.\n101:- [ ] Use visible descendant text in accessible-name precedence and preserve hidden-child and locale behavior.\n102:- [ ] Generate one typed pseudo-state table for indexing, mutation, matching, invalidation, diagnostics, and tests.\n103:- [ ] Make compiler inheritance matching consume the actual element state.\n104:- [ ] Make retained layout transactional, preserve last-known-good geometry, and retry after failure.\n105:- [ ] Use one stateful input dispatcher with pointer capture, press-origin identity, activation semantics, focus eligibility, and invalidation output.\n106:- [ ] Generate element types, source/runtime tags, contracts, events, attributes, style hooks, and accessibility defaults from one schema.\n107:- [ ] Detect CSS custom-property cycles with structured diagnostics and specified invalid-value fallback.\n108:- [ ] Preserve explicit/inherited property masks through retained style resolution and targeted restyle.\n109:- [ ] Derive accessibility focus from canonical live focus state during snapshot generation.\n110:- [ ] Include all shaping inputs and resource/measurer revisions in text measurement contexts and cache keys.\n111:- [ ] Make popover placement tokens and trigger/surface relationships typed, validated, and observable across promotion.\n112:- [ ] Introduce an immutable frame snapshot with phase stamps, semantic diffs, stable identities, and property-based invariant tests.\n114:### 9. Interaction and motion\n118:- [ ] Share visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and target filtering across interaction, rendering, focus, scrolling, tooltips, and accessibility.\n119:- [ ] Use one affine transform/clip contract for hit testing, paint bounds, scrolling, and focus geometry.\n120:- [ ] Prevent disabled and inert nodes, including descendants and captured targets, from receiving activation.\n121:- [ ] Preserve keyframe progress across pause/resume and iteration-boundary changes.\n122:- [ ] Add a `MotionPolicy` snapshot for reduced motion across transitions, keyframes, scrolling, inertia, tooltips, and surfaces.\n123:- [ ] Consolidate focus, pointer capture, press origin, gesture ownership, and scroll ownership into one transaction.\n124:- [ ] Preserve the previous discrete value during visibility transitions until the transition completes.\n125:- [ ] Propagate validated per-keyframe easing through component parsing, shell state, and animation sampling.\n126:- [ ] Keep interaction policy in a renderer-neutral frame/state-machine contract with typed decisions and dirty outputs.\n127:- [ ] Implement `box-shadow` parsing with structured errors or reject it before the public animation API.\n128:- [ ] Give animation instances stable identity and explicit replacement, cancellation, and reversal semantics.\n129:- [ ] Introduce an `InteractionFrame` shared by input, state, style invalidation, layout, animation, paint, and semantics.\n131:### 10. Frontend compiler and host\n135:- [ ] Reject absolute, traversal, and symlinked frontend entrypoint/import paths outside the module root.\n136:- [ ] Keep service payloads Rust-owned unless the consumer has the resolved read capability; event subscriptions alone must not expose state.\n137:- [ ] Reload primary and contribution roots together as one atomic catalog generation.\n138:- [ ] Validate contribution roots against interface requirements, availability, and version ranges.\n139:- [ ] Validate root and nested expression scopes uniformly using parser/runtime symbol tables and source spans.\n140:- [ ] Compile expressions once into shared semantics so preview, live, translation, and composition paths behave identically.\n141:- [ ] Split the frontend host into a renderer-neutral ABI and shell adapters with typed capability-scoped effects.\n142:- [ ] Build one reverse dependency graph over primary and contribution roots for invalidation.\n143:- [ ] Scope local component aliases by owner and canonical source, rejecting collisions.\n144:- [ ] Replace line-oriented Luau symbol discovery with parser/compiler metadata or remove it as a source of truth.\n145:- [ ] Make expression scanning UTF-8 safe and diagnose non-ASCII input instead of panicking.\n146:- [ ] Publish runtime props transactionally so Rust and Luau cannot diverge after a failed update.\n147:- [ ] Preserve typed diagnostic categories and source spans through AST, compiler, shell, LSP, and debug paths.\n148:- [ ] Prevent stale catalog rollback from overwriting a newer generation using a coordinator or compare-and-swap.\n149:- [ ] Enforce distinct popover and overflow child-surface lifecycle, focus, dismissal, placement, and ownership semantics.\n150:- [ ] Dispatch frontend `mount` and `unmount` hooks in every initialization, reload, deactivation, and replacement path.\n151:- [ ] Contain expression/runtime failures with last-known-good trees or bounded error placeholders and actionable diagnostics.\n152:- [ ] Publish normalized public-prop schemas and validate imported/contribution props at the import boundary.\n153:- [ ] Reject local/module import alias collisions or resolve them through one typed import namespace.\n154:- [ ] Introduce a coherent revisioned `FrontendFrame` boundary for tree, catalog, runtime, services, invalidation, diagnostics, and effects.\n155:- [ ] Emit immutable compiled frontend revisions and authorize typed effects only when catalog/runtime revisions still match.\n157:### 11. Luau runtime and sandbox\n161:- [ ] Enforce one sandbox/resource policy for every Luau realm, including instruction, memory, output, queue, storage, and child-process budgets.\n162:- [ ] Keep service state Rust-owned, generation-aware, and capability-filtered instead of exposing shared globals.\n163:- [ ] Route every backend early return, callback failure, stop, and crash through idempotent cleanup and one terminal lifecycle record.\n164:- [ ] Make unsubscribe-safe event iteration and independent subscriber failure reporting explicit.\n165:- [ ] Give stream subprocesses stable identities, bounded queues, exit events, awaited reaping, and shutdown semantics.\n166:- [ ] Move `mesh.exec` off the async backend loop and enforce cancellation, deadlines, executable policy, and output limits.\n167:- [ ] Replace basename executable grants with canonical path/argument capability policy.\n168:- [ ] Move default runtime storage to secure durable XDG state with permissions, quotas, atomic recovery, and revisioned writers.\n169:- [ ] Make backend command dispatch typed, correlated, transactional, coalescable, generation-aware, and bounded.\n170:- [ ] Gate event and side-effect queues through one typed authorization/resource boundary before acceptance.\n171:- [ ] Represent locale as a host-owned per-context cell updated with the translation snapshot.\n172:- [ ] Treat Luau `nil` writes as explicit deletions in Rust state and dependency invalidation.\n173:- [ ] Restrict provider event publication to provider-owned handles and isolate subscriber callback failures.\n174:- [ ] Preserve one stable backend `self` and event/storage handles for each runtime generation.\n175:- [ ] Publish validated initial provider state before atomically marking the provider ready.\n176:- [ ] Compile and swap a fresh Luau environment on reload, preserving only explicit durable storage.\n177:- [ ] Stage backend top-level execution so host side effects cannot escape a failed `start(self)`.\n178:- [ ] Bound command/event ingress, JSON depth/bytes, event counts, and aggregate runtime resource budgets.\n179:- [ ] Separate effective read and control grants and enforce them through both proxy and shell paths.\n180:- [ ] Reconcile changed poll intervals after every stream callback.\n181:- [ ] Return recoverable host-installation errors instead of panicking during backend setup.\n182:- [ ] Introduce a per-module `RuntimeSession` combining realm, host, resource broker, lifecycle, state, supervisor, health, backoff, and quarantine.\n184:### 12. Rendering and paint\n188:- [ ] Make `PixelCanvasSession` storage-safe so mapped drawing surfaces cannot outlive backing allocations.\n189:- [ ] Lower asymmetric four-edge borders and four-corner radii correctly.\n190:- [ ] Use one cumulative affine transform/clip model across paint, damage, blur, descendants, and interaction.\n191:- [ ] Lower node opacity and blend mode as isolated compositing groups instead of per primitive.\n192:- [ ] Include paint-order/topology changes in display-list generations and stable equal-z ordering.\n193:- [ ] Unify dirty contracts and paint signatures for all content/style fields, including controls, text, icons, and variables.\n194:- [ ] Include font/resource revisions in text style painting and all glyph/font/text cache keys.\n195:- [ ] Make backdrop blur an explicit renderer/backend capability with validated fallback behavior.\n196:- [ ] Use one fractional-scale rounding policy across layout, buffer, paint, copy, and protocol damage.\n197:- [ ] Preserve release batch/barrier metrics in diagnostics and debug telemetry.\n198:- [ ] Validate caller lineage before trusting generation shortcuts.\n199:- [ ] Include resource revisions in retained-present decisions and cache invalidation.\n200:- [ ] Preserve pending damage and avoid acknowledging unshown frames after presentation errors.\n201:- [ ] Move resource decode and rasterization off the frame thread through a bounded resource broker.\n202:- [ ] Enforce byte- and dimension-based cache budgets for decoded assets, fonts, glyphs, text, Skia, PixelBuffer, and SHM allocations.\n203:- [ ] Introduce a typed frame paint plan with immutable inputs, topology, transforms, effect regions, replay spans, and exact logical/device damage.\n205:### 13. Surface policy and configuration\n209:- [ ] Enforce the author-only `promotable` guard for settings, IPC, and live role changes.\n210:- [ ] Remove or formally protect `promotable` from user-overridable/ejected settings.\n211:- [ ] Validate manifest surface enums and role constraints with canonical parsers before graph resolution.\n212:- [ ] Include blur and window decorations in semantic presentation change detection.\n213:- [ ] Route settings-driven role reloads through the same transactional transition supervisor as explicit promotion.\n214:- [ ] Share role-field metadata across manifest diagnostics, settings validation, ejection, and protocol lowering.\n215:- [ ] Preserve localization identity and distinguish effective values from overrides during configuration ejection.\n216:- [ ] Use typed unmeasured/content/surface/wire extents at the shell/presentation boundary.\n217:- [ ] Replace split surface field lists with a revisioned semantic policy diff and generation.\n218:- [ ] Build the policy compiler producing declared contracts, effective snapshots, and typed transition plans.\n220:### 14. Wayland platform and presentation\n224:- [ ] Return typed create/configure/present/lost outcomes, cache only accepted generations, and retain damage for missing surfaces.\n225:- [ ] Route close, dismiss, parent destruction, role replacement, and connection loss through one idempotent teardown supervisor.\n226:- [ ] Replace incomplete presentation hashes and warm region caches with typed semantic diffs and object/configure/frame generations.\n227:- [ ] Preserve click-grab seat and serial identity through input normalization, popup requests, and one-shot configuration.\n228:- [ ] Gate popup reposition by negotiated protocol version and validate popup role, parent, identity, and reparenting.\n229:- [ ] Use one authoritative logical extent for paint, buffer validation, viewport, regions, attach, and queries.\n230:- [ ] Commit opaque, blur, and other surface-state changes even when pixel damage is empty.\n231:- [ ] Separate frame-callback waiting from buffer-release backpressure and prevent hot retry loops.\n232:- [ ] Make input ownership per seat and cancel pointer, touch, gesture, focus, and repeat transactions during teardown.\n233:- [ ] Validate buffer length/stride/scale, propagate attach/region errors, expose connection loss, and reap clipboard children.\n234:- [ ] Replace the recorder backend with a deterministic protocol-state lifecycle simulator plus a focused live compositor matrix.\n235:- [ ] Add full pointer-button identity and IME/text-input-v3 support while preserving normalized Escape behavior.\n236:- [ ] Build the transactional, capability-aware presentation engine with version reporting, reactive popups, multi-output membership, per-seat input, and shared SHM/dmabuf/GPU frame contracts.\n238:### 15. Shell core and orchestration\n242:- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one immutable activation plan and atomic runtime-generation commit.\n243:- [ ] Reconcile backend enable/disable and every graph delta through the same activation coordinator.\n244:- [ ] Tag backend tasks, bridges, messages, events, results, and restart deadlines with activation generations and provider epochs.\n245:- [ ] Make frontend/backend stop, unmount, storage flush, worker join, and shutdown cleanup explicit and idempotent.\n246:- [ ] Give detached workers owned wake handles and lifecycle guards that outlive the eventfd until join.\n247:- [ ] Isolate component/runtime callback, tick, build, render, and reload failures with placeholders, diagnostics, and quarantine.\n248:- [ ] Replace static startup watching with a managed generation-aware watch set and bounded polling fallback.\n249:- [ ] Process CoreRequest effects through one fair bounded scheduler with causal budgets and cycle detection.\n250:- [ ] Publish provider unavailable/recovery transitions from committed provider generations.\n251:- [ ] Route control-plane writes through declared durable revisions and ordered settings/theme/locale effect batches.\n252:- [ ] Distinguish intentional legacy/no-profile operation from invalid configured graph/profile recovery.\n253:- [ ] Connect package journal commit/rollback to runtime activation so disk and live state share one recoverable transaction.\n254:- [ ] Introduce `ActiveSnapshot` with candidate preview in hidden surfaces and an explicit quiescing-to-stopped shutdown state machine.\n256:### 16. Developer and authoring tools\n260:- [ ] Make uninstall path-safe and reject traversal/symlink escapes before recursive deletion.\n261:- [ ] Make package mutations atomic, journaled, crash-recoverable, and failure-injection tested.\n262:- [ ] Make live profile switching use typed acknowledgements and exact-generation recovery.\n263:- [ ] Make CLI and shell use one package ownership and transaction contract.\n264:- [ ] Refresh one canonical graph-authoring snapshot for CLI, doctor, LSP, and runtime consumers.\n265:- [ ] Generate LSP manifest/schema validation from runtime contracts instead of duplicated under-approximations.\n266:- [ ] Convert all LSP positions to UTF-16 code units at the protocol boundary.\n267:- [ ] Make `--replace` and CLI flags enforce their documented typed behavior.\n268:- [ ] Make LSP analysis syntax-aware with recoverable partial ASTs and useful mid-edit diagnostics.\n269:- [ ] Replace heuristic Luau/JSON scanners with standards-compliant parsers and source-span data.\n270:- [ ] Support workspace-folder-only initialization and versioned `didChange` refresh generations.\n271:- [ ] Select manifest flavors from parsed structure, not textual substring checks.\n272:- [ ] Make hover consume the current registry and expose service field/command documentation.\n273:- [ ] Restrict definition resolution to existing module-contained imports.\n274:- [ ] Preserve Unicode JSON escapes, registry generations, and secure import provenance in authoring diagnostics.\n276:## Foundation contracts\n278:## Module system\n284:- [ ] Close module filesystem escapes before any package mutation or source\n287:- [ ] Make installed-graph activation fail closed and build catalogs/provider\n290:- [ ] Replace the duplicate CLI/shell package implementations with one locked,\n293:- [ ] Enforce required/optional module dependencies, module/interface version\n296:- [ ] Make the canonical `module.json` loader the only production path; legacy\n299:- [ ] Make module lifecycle and health authoritative across graph, frontend, and\n302:- [ ] Align the lock schema with the specification and populate dependency and\n305:- [ ] Move the remaining built-in debug and theme/locale service behavior\n309:- [ ] **Deferred — unify the four contribution schemas.** Theme, icons, i18n,\n316:## Service contracts\n318:- [ ] Replace the additive interface registry with one immutable, atomic\n321:- [ ] Compile and enforce complete contracts end to end: canonical unique names,\n324:- [ ] Make service methods correlated request/response transactions with typed\n327:- [ ] Gate provider/profile commits on validated readiness and buffered initial\n330:- [ ] Diff the complete compiled contract in consumer and provider directions,\n334:## Themes\n336:- [ ] Make graph-authorized theme descriptors the only catalog/loader, with\n339:- [ ] Make theme selection, profile switching, graph changes, and hot reload one\n342:- [ ] Share the restricted CSS/token/keyframe lowering path between themes and\n345:- [ ] Complete `mesh.theme` and settings with modes, sparse token overrides,\n349:## Localization / i18n\n351:- [ ] Replace per-surface locale engines and the cross-module global key pool\n354:- [ ] Make locale selection a durable revisioned settings/profile transaction:\n357:- [ ] Compile bounded typed catalogs with per-entry diagnostics, interpolation,\n360:- [ ] Route templates, Luau, manifests, props, generated settings, and debug data\n363:- [ ] Drive locale CLI/LSP/doctor, extraction, missing-key and provenance tooling\n367:## Host resources and icon packs\n369:- [ ] Replace discovery-time global resource registration with one immutable,\n372:- [ ] Make icon resolution deterministic and complete: canonical owner-scoped\n375:- [ ] Complete font-pack runtime support with logical and pack-qualified roles,\n378:- [ ] Unify XDG and font discovery into a refreshable host catalog; propagate\n381:- [ ] Remove legacy icon config and discovery authorities, and drive bounded\n385:## Component language\n387:- [ ] Replace lossy `.mesh` block extraction and ad-hoc template preprocessing\n390:- [ ] Make component compilation resolve imports per owner, enforce module-root\n393:- [ ] Make the `<props>` contract one normalized typed value pipeline: reject\n397:## UI element core\n399:- [ ] Establish unique `NodeId` tree identity and reject duplicate/unknown\n401:- [ ] Generate element contracts, runtime types, attributes, events, and\n403:- [ ] Consolidate interaction, style invalidation, retained layout, and\n406:- [ ] Normalize ARIA/visibility/name semantics after child construction and\n409:## Interaction and motion\n411:- [ ] Unify visibility, transformed geometry, disabled/inert eligibility, and\n414:- [ ] Define a canonical interaction transaction for focus, pointer capture,\n417:- [ ] Complete motion semantics: pause/resume, stable animation identity,\n420:- [ ] Implement or safely gate the public `box-shadow` parser and add the\n423:## Frontend compiler and host\n425:- [ ] Compile, validate, watch, invalidate, and reload primary and\n428:- [ ] Gate service payload publication on capabilities and replace the\n431:- [ ] Complete frontend runtime lifecycle and recovery: dispatch mount/unmount,\n434:- [ ] Unify template expression semantics and root-scope validation around the\n437:- [ ] Split the renderer/Wayland/package/debug policy out of the\n440:## Shell core and orchestration\n442:- [ ] Replace split profile/runtime mutation with one revisioned activation\n446:- [ ] Reconcile every live graph delta, including backend module enable/disable;\n449:- [ ] Add explicit frontend unmount and graceful backend stop/join, then route\n--- status tail / next locale context ---\n35-generated component settings use the owning module without global key leakage.\n36-Locale selection is now normalized and immutable at the snapshot boundary:\n37-BCP 47 tags derive parent and terminal fallback chains, direction and revision\n38:are published with the locale service state, and invalid runtime tags are\n39-rejected. Typed catalog compilation now preserves valid siblings, validates\n40-placeholder parity, and resolves bounded plural/select entries in Rust and\n41-Luau. LocaleEngine now publishes revisioned immutable catalog Arcs with an\n42-explicit replacement seam. Graph discovery, cached graph loading,\n43-module enable/disable, provider selection, profile switching, and settings\n44:locale reloads prepare complete catalog candidates before committing selection,\n45-graph, profile, and runtime state; failed reads/parses retain the\n46-last-known-good graph and catalog. Shell-created components consume the\n47-committed Arc instead of rereading graph files, while standalone component\n48:fixtures retain their source-backed fallback. The next localization item is\n49:ordered language-pack chains, per-module defaults, winning-source provenance,\n50-and duplicate precedence checks.\n51-\n52-The first four Section 2 items are complete. Package commits publish\n--\n210-\n211-Section 01 improvement 2 is complete: shell operations now pass through one\n212-typed registry that validates payloads, required capabilities, and caller\n213:constraints. Raw shell events, locale writes, and popover helpers share that\n214-authorization path; malformed, unauthorized, unknown, and dropped requests\n215-produce structured diagnostics.\n216-\n--- planning rules ---\n# MESH planning\n\nProgress tracking for MESH. Plain Markdown, updated by hand, no tooling — if a\nfile here needs a program to stay correct, it is the wrong shape.\n\nThis directory answers three questions, and each has exactly one home:\n\n| Question | File | Shape |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What am I doing right now? | [`STATUS.md`](STATUS.md) | One page. Overwritten as work moves. |\n| What is still open? | [`../docs/BACKLOG.md`](../docs/BACKLOG.md) | Terse checklist. Items leave when done. |\n| What happened, and what did it measure? | [`log/`](log/) | Append-only, dated. Never edited after the fact. |\n\nThe split exists because the old backlog tried to be all three at once and grew\nto 1,250 lines, with individual items carrying hundred-line progress narratives.\nA backlog that records history stops being scannable, and history that lives\ninside a checklist gets silently rewritten.\n\n## The rules\n\n1. **The backlog says what is open, not what happened.** An item is one to three\n   lines: what to do, and why it is not done. No dated progress paragraphs, no\n   benchmark numbers. If an item needs detail, link to a log entry.\n2. **Finishing an item removes it from the backlog.** Its record goes to the\n   log. `[x]` entries do not accumulate in `docs/BACKLOG.md`; a completed item\n   that stays visible is just history in the wrong file.\n3. **The log is append-only.** Correct a wrong entry with a new dated entry that\n   says so. Never revise a past one — the numbers in it are the baseline\n   something later gets compared against.\n4. **Every performance claim carries its measurement.** Machine, build profile,\n   workload shape, before and after, and the gate name if one exists. A speedup\n   without its workload cannot be reproduced or trusted.\n5. **Record what failed.** An approach that was measured and reverted is worth\n   more than one that was never tried, because it stops the next person\n   repeating it. See the rejected-experiments table in\n   [`log/performance-log.md`](log/performance-log.md).\n6. **`STATUS.md` is disposable.** It describes the present. Overwrite it freely;\n   the log is what remembers.\n\n## Layout\n\n```\n.planning/\n  README.md      this file — how the system works\n  STATUS.md      current position: in flight, next, blocked\n  log/           dated history; append-only\n  archive/       frozen; superseded or tool-era records\n  todos/         standalone plans too big for a backlog line\n  codebase/      structural analysis of the source tree\n  research/      external investigation feeding decisions\n  spikes/        throwaway experiments and their conclusions\n  prototypes/    working code kept for reference\n  debug/         recorded debugging investigations\n  notes/         everything that outlived its conversation\n  frontend/  renderer/  seeds/\n```\n\n### `log/`\n\nDated history. See [`log/README.md`](log/README.md) for what goes where and the\nentry format. Current files:\n\n- `2026-07.md` — the running monthly work log.\n- `performance-log.md` — performance history and the rejected-experiments\n  table, back to 2026-07-02.\n- `backlog-archive-2026-07-28.md` — verbatim snapshot of the pre-split backlog,\n  holding the detailed progress narratives for items still open.\n- `sections.md` — the current package-oriented concern map; section letters\n  (A–V) in older records are historical references.\n\n### `archive/`\n\nFrozen. Nothing here is maintained and nothing here is authoritative — it is\nkept because it explains how the project arrived where it is. See\n[`archive/README.md`](archive/README.md).\n\n### `todos/`\n\nA backlog line that needs a design before it can be executed gets a file in\n`todos/pending/`, and the backlog line links to it. Moves to `todos/completed/`\nwhen the work lands, with the outcome appended.\n\n## Relationship to `docs/`\n\n`docs/` is the contract: [`docs/spec/`](../docs/spec/) defines behavior and\nmarks each section `Shipped` or `Target`; the guides describe how things\ncurrently work. `.planning/` is evidence and intent. When the two disagree about\nwhat MESH *does*, the spec wins — a planning document may explain why a decision\nwas made, but it never overrides the specification.\n--- localization spec ---\n# 07 — Localization\n\n> Part of the [MESH Specification](README.md).\n\nLocalization is **module-authored and user-extensible**: every module ships\ntranslations for its own strings; **language packs** layer additional or\nreplacement catalogs on top (ordered multi-active chain, like icon packs);\nthe user picks a locale and fallback chain. Lookups go through the\n`mesh.locale` interface — the same contract-and-registry model as everything\nelse.\n\n**Status: shipped** (catalogs, `t()`, locale switching, fallback,\nmanifest-localized text); language-pack layering and some formatting surface\nare **target** where marked.\n\n## 1. Scoping\n\nTranslations are **scoped by module id**: `results.files` in\n`@mesh/launcher` never collides with `results.files` in another module. This\nis what makes third-party packs safe — they target modules individually.\nInterface modules may also own catalogs (shared domain strings).\n\n## 2. Module-bundled catalogs\n\n```json\n\"mesh\": {\n  \"i18n\": { \"defaultLocale\": \"en\", \"supportedLocales\": [\"en\", \"sk\"] },\n  \"provides\": {\n    \"i18n\": [\n      { \"id\": \"en\", \"locale\": \"en\", \"path\": \"config/i18n/en.json\" },\n      { \"id\": \"sk\", \"locale\": \"sk\", \"path\": \"config/i18n/sk.json\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `mesh.i18n.supportedLocales` is install metadata (which language assets an\n  installer may fetch/enable); `defaultLocale` is the module's terminal\n  fallback and belongs in `supportedLocales`.\n- `mesh.provides.i18n` lists the concrete bundled files. Any module kind may\n  bundle catalogs for its own strings.\n\n## 3. Language packs\n\nA `language-pack` module contributes catalogs *for other modules* using the\nsame contribution shape, one entry per target module:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"@community/cs-language-pack\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"mesh\": {\n    \"apiVersion\": \"0.1\",\n    \"kind\": \"language-pack\",\n    \"provides\": {\n      \"i18n\": [\n        { \"id\": \"launcher-cs\", \"locale\": \"cs\", \"module\": \"@mesh/launcher\",\n          \"path\": \"translations/@mesh/launcher/cs.json\" },\n        { \"id\": \"panel-cs\", \"locale\": \"cs\", \"module\": \"@mesh/panel\",\n          \"path\": \"translations/@mesh/panel/cs.json\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n*(Status: target — the `module` targeting field and pack layering.)* Multiple\npacks coexist as an ordered chain; catalogs from packs shadow module-bundled\ncatalogs key-by-key. Community translators can localize modules they don't\nown; when a module renames keys, packs targeting it need updates.\n\n## 4. Catalog format\n\nFlat JSON, key → string; dotted keys are organizational, lookup treats them\nas opaque.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"search.placeholder\": \"Hľadať aplikácie\",\n  \"battery.charging\": \"Nabíja sa, {time_to_full}\",\n  \"notifications.count\": {\n    \"_plural\": true,\n    \"one\": \"{count} upozornenie\", \"few\": \"{count} upozornenia\",\n    \"many\": \"{count} upozornení\", \"other\": \"{count} upozornení\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- **Interpolation**: `{name}` substitutes `args.name`; no expression\n  evaluation in strings.\n- **Plurals**: `_plural: true` objects with CLDR categories; `other`\n  required.\n\n## 5. Lookup chain\n\nFor `(module_id, key)`:\n\n1. User-pinned override (settings store; rare, for corrections)\n2. Language packs providing `(module_id, active_locale)`, chain order\n3. Module-bundled catalog for the active locale\n4. Repeat 2–3 for each locale in the user's fallback chain\n5. Module-bundled `defaultLocale` catalog\n6. Visible miss: the key rendered as `!!key` plus a diagnostic\n\nLocale codes are BCP 47; matching is most-specific-first (`sk-SK` satisfies\n`sk`; not vice versa unless the chain says so). User settings\n([08](08-settings.md)):\n\n```json\n\"shell\": { \"locale\": { \"active\": \"sk-SK\", \"chain\": [\"sk-SK\", \"sk\", \"en\"] } }\n```\n\nWhen `chain` is omitted it derives from `active` (drop region → drop script →\n`en`). Changing locale emits `LocaleChanged`; every subscriber refreshes.\n\n## 6. Consumption surfaces\n\n**Scripts** — the i18n library and locale interface:\n\n```luau\nlocal t = import(\"mesh.i18n\", \"t\")\nlocal loc = require(\"mesh.locale\")\n\nlabel = t(\"search.placeholder\")                 -- module-scoped key\nloc.format_number(1234567.89)                    -- \"1 234 567,89\" in sk-SK\nloc.format_date(os.time(), \"short\")\nloc.format_duration(3675)                        -- \"1 h 1 min\"\n```\n\nModules never hand-format numbers/dates/durations — CLDR rules differ per\nlocale. *(Formatting surface beyond `t`: target.)*\n\n**Manifests** — field-local localized text objects wherever a manifest field\nshould be catalog-backed (keybind labels, prop labels):\n\n```json\n\"label\": { \"t\": \"keybind.mute.label\", \"fallback\": \"Mute audio\" }\n```\n\nRaw strings in manifest text fields are literals, never implicit keys.\n`fallback` is required and used when no catalog resolves; misses are\nnon-fatal diagnostics with module id, field path, key, and fallback.\n\n**Props** — `label`/`description`/enum labels in `<props>` are\n`LocalizedText` resolved the same way ([03 §3](03-components.md)). The\ninstalled graph scans `.mesh` sources for `t()` keys and reports missing\ncatalog entries.\n\n## 7. RTL, bidi, and script fallback\n\n- `locale.dir` exposes `\"ltr\"`/`\"rtl\"`; surfaces flip automatically for RTL\n  locales. Authors use logical properties (`margin-inline-start`), not\n  physical ones.\n- Font fallback for scripts the active fonts lack is the renderer's job\n  (cosmic-text); themes may declare script-specific stacks later\n  ([06 §8](06-fonts.md)).\n\n*(Status: target.)*\n\n## 8. Tooling\n\n```\nmesh locale list                    # locales available (bundled + packs)\nmesh locale active                  # current locale + chain\nmesh locale set <code>\nmesh locale which <module> <key>    # which layer supplied the string\nmesh locale missing <module>        # keys with no catalog entry\nmesh locale extract <module>        # translator template with every used key\n```\n--- latest log locale entries ---\n1-# August 2026\n2-\n3:## 2026-08-22 — Commit locale catalog snapshots with graph and profile state\n4-\n5:`working tree` · area: graph/profile/locale transactions\n6-\n7:Graph candidates now prepare every contributed catalog into one immutable,\n8-revisioned `LocaleEngine` candidate before discovery, cached loading, module\n9-enable/disable, provider-selection, or backend-replacement commits. Profile\n10:switch candidates retain the prepared locale engine until frontend and backend\n11:readiness, and settings locale reloads reject invalid selection/catalog\n12-candidates before changing the active settings snapshot. Shell-created\n13:components consume that same catalog `Arc`; standalone component fixtures keep\n14:their local source-backed compatibility path. Any catalog read or JSON parse\n15:failure leaves the previous graph, selection, and catalog snapshot active.\n16-\n17:**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n18-(11 tests), `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-theme` passed (23\n19-tests), `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-config --lib` passed (41\n20-tests), and formatting/whitespace checks pass. The shell regression is present\n21-but cannot run because the pre-existing\n22-`InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n23-`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229` stops compilation before\n24-`mesh-core-shell`.\n25-\n26:## 2026-08-22 — Prepare graph locale catalogs before surface commit\n27-\n28:`working tree` · area: surface catalog lifecycle\n29-\n30:Frontend surfaces now compile graph catalogs into a fresh locale candidate and\n31:replace their catalog Arc only after all catalog assets are readable. An asset\n32:read/parse failure retains the prior catalog snapshot while valid structured\n33-entries continue to produce per-entry diagnostics.\n34-\n35-**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and\n36:`git diff --check` pass. The focused locale suite passed 9 tests before this\n37-shell-only wiring; workspace compilation remains blocked by pre-existing\n38-service/module/scripting errors before the shell crate.\n39-\n40:## 2026-08-22 — Establish immutable locale catalog snapshots\n41-\n42:`working tree` · area: catalog snapshot ownership\n43-\n44:`LocaleEngine` now stores catalogs behind a revisioned immutable\n45-`LocaleCatalogSnapshot` Arc. Catalog loads use copy-on-write preparation, and\n46:callers can atomically replace the catalog pointer while readers retain the\n47-previous last-known-good snapshot. The shell/graph/profile coordinator still\n48-needs to adopt this seam; that remaining work stays open in the backlog.\n49-\n50:**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n51-(9 tests), `git diff --check` passed. The final formatting check was retried\n52-after the Nix daemon socket was temporarily unavailable; earlier focused\n53-format checks pass for the same working tree.\n54-\n55:## 2026-08-22 — Compile typed locale entries without sibling loss\n56-\n57:`working tree` · area: typed catalogs and shared message rendering\n58-\n59:Locale catalog loading now compiles bounded string, plural, and select entries\n60-independently. Invalid keys, malformed placeholders, inconsistent variant\n61-placeholders, unknown plural categories, and oversized entries produce\n62-per-entry diagnostics while valid siblings remain available. Rust translators\n63-share interpolation and plural/select resolution, and Luau `t(key, values)`\n64:uses the same compiled entries. Graph catalog loading now accepts structured\n65-JSON instead of rejecting an entire file at the first plural object.\n66-\n67:**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n68-(8 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n69---check` pass. The scripting/shell workspace remains blocked by pre-existing\n70-closure ownership and module graph compilation errors before shell compilation.\n71-\n72:## 2026-08-22 — Normalize locale selection into revisioned snapshots\n73-\n74:`working tree` · area: locale selection and service state\n75-\n76-Added immutable `LocaleSelection` snapshots with canonical BCP 47 casing,\n77-derived script/region parents, explicit terminal fallback, RTL/LTR direction,\n78-and monotonic revisions. Locale changes now replace the prior chain instead of\n79:accumulating stale locales; invalid runtime tags are rejected. The shell\n80-publishes the normalized chain, direction, and revision, and retained\n81-components adopt the same selection snapshot.\n82-\n83:**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n84-(7 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n85---check` pass. Workspace compilation remains blocked by pre-existing errors in\n86-the service, module, and scripting crates before shell compilation.\n87-\n88:## 2026-08-22 — Replace global locale lookup with owner-scoped translators\n89-\n90-`working tree` · area: localization ownership and consumption\n91-\n92:`LocaleEngine` no longer merges module catalogs into a shared translation\n93-pool. It now exposes explicit module and core translator handles; module\n94:translators resolve only their owner’s catalogs. Template variable stores,\n95-Luau i18n maps, manifest text, debug metadata, and generated composition prop\n96-settings all use the corresponding owner-scoped handle. Existing keybind/debug\n97:fixtures were migrated to module-owned catalogs, and cross-module/core leakage\n98:is covered by locale tests.\n99-\n100:**Verification.** `nix develop --command cargo test -p mesh-core-locale` passed\n101-(4 tests), `nix develop --command cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff\n102---check` pass. The scripting/shell compile remains blocked by the pre-existing\n103-`InterfaceProvider` dereference error at\n104-`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`.\n--\n121-`crates/core/extension/service/src/interface.rs:229`.\n122-\n123-## 2026-08-22 — Make active theme snapshots immutable and identities deterministic\n124-\n125:`working tree` · area: theme snapshot identity and catalog registration\n126-\n127-ThemeEngine now keeps an `Arc` of the active immutable snapshot and refreshes it\n128-only after controlled active-theme edits or replacement commits. The unrestricted\n129-mutable active-theme accessor was removed; duplicate, whitespace-ambiguous, and\n--\n159-\n160-Profile switches prepare graph-selected theme sources before committing the\n161-candidate runtime. Reload parsing and component invalidation failures now keep\n162-the prior theme/watch snapshot and record `theme_reload_rejected`; successful\n163:profile theme changes preserve the existing catalog while replacing only the\n164-prepared active snapshot.\n165-\n166-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\n167-Cargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\n--\n170-## 2026-08-21 — Open theme selections through contained graph sources\n171-\n172-`73dcc5ad` · area: theme source security and selection\n173-\n174:Theme selection now accepts only graph-scoped catalog identities. Their mode\n175-sources open beneath the owning module root with no-follow directory/file\n176-handles, bounded reads, regular-file checks, and UTF-8 validation; registration\n177-and reload use the same boundary. Legacy free-form selection paths are rejected\n178-by the shell control path.\n--\n180-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\n181-Cargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\n182-network access is unavailable.\n183-\n184:## 2026-08-21 — Build the graph-authorized theme catalog\n185-\n186:`447d2b43` · area: theme catalog and module graph\n187-\n188:Enabled canonical theme contributions now form one deterministic catalog keyed\n189-by owner-scoped identity. Descriptors retain owner module, label, ordered modes,\n190-validated default mode, and a module-root-relative source handle. Runtime theme\n191:registration consumes that catalog instead of scanning `config/themes`.\n192-\n193-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\n194-Cargo tests remain blocked because the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and\n195-network access is unavailable.\n--\n241-`working tree` · area: service contract capability and provider selection\n242-\n243-Contracts now compile explicit `capabilities.featureGroups` entries with\n244-required/optional semantics and normalized capability lists. The interface\n245:registry and immutable catalog track provider feature advertisements per\n246-provider, enable matching groups, tolerate unavailable optional groups, and\n247-reject providers missing required groups. Lookup diagnostics explain required\n248-feature-group gaps, and compatibility diffs classify optional additions as\n249-additive and required additions as breaking.\n--\n267-access is unavailable.\n268-\n269-## 2026-08-21 — Publish immutable compiled service contracts\n270-\n271:`working tree` · area: service contract compilation and catalog authority\n272-\n273-Service contracts now compile into an immutable, shared `CompiledContract`\n274-artifact. It owns canonical identity, normalized recursive schemas, unique\n275-declaration symbols, operation capability policy, behavioral transport metadata,\n276-stable schema/policy/behavior fingerprints, and declaration provenance. Mutable\n277:registration and immutable catalog snapshots publish the same `Arc` artifact;\n278-resolutions retain a raw compatibility view for callers still migrating.\n279-\n280-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\n281-Cargo checks could not start because the local registry lacks `sha2` and network\n--\n374-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Cargo\n375-checks and focused tests could not start because the local registry lacks\n376-`sha2` and network access is unavailable.\n377-\n378:## 2026-08-21 — Publish generation-stamped resolved service catalogs\n379-\n380-`working tree` · area: service registry and runtime interface bindings\n381-\n382-Interface registration now uses one lock-protected contract/provider snapshot,\n383-so each resolution observes a coherent epoch instead of reading independent\n384-locks. Snapshots carry a monotonic generation and are exposed as immutable\n385-`ResolvedServiceCatalog` views; frontend discovery, profile startup, and debug\n386:inspection now consume those views. Existing catalog consumers remain source\n387-compatible while the mutable registry stays confined to the registration\n388-boundary, and regression coverage verifies old snapshots do not change after a\n389-later provider registration.\n390-\n--\n511-dependency.\n512-\n513-## 2026-08-21 — Isolate frontend source indexing failures\n514-\n515:`working tree` · area: frontend component catalog\n516-\n517:Frontend catalog construction now stays separate from the resolved module\n518-graph. Primary `.mesh` roots and extension-point contributions compile as\n519-independent incremental entries, unchanged entries are reused by manifest and\n520-watched-source fingerprints, and a failed module or contribution is retained\n521:as a scoped catalog diagnostic instead of aborting unrelated entries. Each\n522-declared contribution source remains in the host watch set so an edit can\n523-retry a previously invalid source without changing graph activation state.\n524-\n525-Verification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\n526:focused catalog regression could not start because `sha2` is absent from the\n527-local registry cache; offline execution reports the same missing dependency.\n528-\n529-## 2026-08-21 — Explain candidate graph effects during dry runs\n530-\n--\n547-`working tree` · area: module graph and shell discovery\n548-\n549-Shell discovery now loads one validated `InstalledModuleGraph` candidate and\n550-constructs runtime module instances, provider registrations, settings schemas,\n551:and frontend catalog inputs from that graph. It no longer discovers arbitrary\n552-module manifests and treats a missing graph as an allow-all filter. Graph load\n553-failure retains the last-known-good runtime; startup with no valid graph\n554-activates no module. Duplicate dependency validation in the shell was removed\n555-so the package graph is the normalized compatibility authority.\n--\n595-## 2026-08-20 — Fail closed graph candidates and retain live activation\n596-\n597-`working tree` · area: module graph activation and shell runtime reloads\n598-\n599:Graph-backed frontend catalogs now compile and mount only enabled modules from\n600-the validated installed graph. Discovery no longer registers module-owned\n601-contracts or providers before graph validation, invalid profile roots fail\n602-closed during startup, and graph replacement uses a prepare/commit boundary so\n603-failed reloads keep the last-known-good graph and active frontend/backend\n604-runtime. Package removal also validates the replacement graph before removing\n605-the in-memory module or deactivating its live surface.\n606-\n607-Verification: the new last-known-good graph reload regression passed; focused\n608:catalog and frontend activation tests passed; `cargo check -p mesh-core-shell`\n609-and formatting checks passed. The full shell library ran 674 passed, 6 failed,\n610-and 130 ignored; the six failures are the known hover, raster, inspector,\n611-backend-manifest, debug-layout-label, and theme-contract baseline failures\n612-recorded by the Section 15 audit. No performance claim is made.\n--\n754-Section 01 improvement 2 is implemented. A typed shell operation registry now\n755-defines the recognized `shell.*` operations, validates their payloads, checks\n756-required capabilities and caller constraints, and gives unknown, malformed,\n757-unauthorized, and dropped requests distinct diagnostic outcomes. Raw shell\n758:events, `mesh.locale.set`, and popover helpers all use the same authorization\n759-function; malformed position margins are rejected instead of being coerced to\n760-zero. Generic interface service commands remain routed through their existing\n761-contract registry after the shell-operation boundary.\n762-\n--\n768-\n769-## 2026-08-20 — Resolve capability approvals before module activation\n770-\n771-Section 01 improvement 1 is implemented. The capability foundation now has a\n772:closed catalog and immutable `EffectiveCapabilities` resolver backed by\n773-explicit `mesh.capabilityApprovals` in the root module graph. Required\n774-capabilities must be approved, optional capabilities are denied unless\n775-explicitly approved, and unknown or malformed declarations fail closed.\n776-\n--\n790-`working tree` · area: `mesh-core-shell` startup, activation, scheduling,\n791-reload, recovery, and shutdown\n792-\n793-Four Luna xhigh passes mapped Section 15 from config/profile discovery through\n794:graph and catalog construction, frontend/backend initialization, message and\n795-effect routing, rendering/presentation, live profile/provider/package changes,\n796-reloads, failure recovery, and shutdown. Independent logic/order, direct\n797-code-error, and transaction/lifecycle reviews confirmed non-atomic profile\n798-activation, ignored root entrypoints, missing live backend enable/disable,\n--\n833-\n834-## 2026-08-20 — Audit frontend compiler and host end to end\n835-\n836-`working tree` · area: `mesh-core-frontend`, `mesh-core-frontend-host`, and\n837:shell/catalog integration\n838-\n839-Four requested review passes mapped Section 10 from module/profile input and\n840:`.mesh` parsing through import and contribution validation, catalog revisions,\n841-runtime/VM lifecycle, expression/style/props lowering, widget-tree composition,\n842-service observations, shell effects, child surfaces, diagnostics, and\n843-presentation. The audit found module-root escapes, service capability leakage,\n844-stale contribution reloads and invalidation, contribution contract gaps, split\n--\n914-deduplicated open work is recorded in the canonical backlog.\n915-\n916-## 2026-08-19 — Audit localization and i18n end to end\n917-\n918:`working tree` · area: locale selection, catalogs, and translation consumers\n919-\n920-Four Luna xhigh passes mapped Section 5, challenged its logical and feature\n921:model, checked concrete defects, and audited catalog, filesystem, capability,\n922-and tooling boundaries. The audit traces graph contributions and settings\n923:through catalog loading, fallback, component/Luau/manifest consumption, runtime\n924-switching, profiles, persistence, reloads, and diagnostics. Its process map,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
